insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('46712f75-47ec-5a35-932e-6825746f926a'::uuid,'c906fb73-66a3-5477-8732-349b15f6e5c4'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.1 – Legal and Institutional Framework\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A1.1** (M, weight 3) City ordinance/EO creating the CDRRMO as a regular office (not ad hoc), with defined functions mirroring RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12; IRR Rule 6; JMC 2014-1\n- **A1.2** (M, weight 2) CDRRMC constituted with complete membership per RA 10121 Sec. 11 (incl. CSO and private sector reps)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 11\n- **A1.3** (M, weight 2) CDRRMC meets at least quarterly (and as needed); minutes and resolutions on file  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 11; GKS/SGLG functionality criteria\n- **A1.5** (GP, weight 2) Local DRRM-related ordinances in force (e.g., pre-emptive/forced evacuation, no-build zones, hazard-based zoning)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(19); RA 7160; CLUP/ZO\n- **A1.6** (M, weight 3) Protocol for declaring a State of Calamity, incl. CDRRMC recommendation steps and post-declaration measures (State of Imminent Disaster now assessed separately under A1.8)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 16\n- **A1.7** (GP, weight 1) DRRM is represented in City Development Council and Local Finance Committee deliberations  \n  *Basis:* RA 7160; JMC 2013-1\n- **A1.8** (M, weight 3) State of Imminent Disaster procedure: CDRRMC coordinates with RDRRMC VIII when the pre-disaster risk assessment shows reasonable certainty of severe impact; request format to RDRRMC (24-hour reply); Mayor''s EO template; adoption rule when the Region (or Province, applicability to an ICC to be confirmed) is declared; lifting grounds and authority  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule II Sec. 6; Rule III Secs. 2-4','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('3f1cb2eb-5bb2-5c0d-bd0b-dc623233dc05'::uuid,'f379ea8e-ae82-5eb6-9887-f700227215ce'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.2 – Office Organization\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A1.4** (M, weight 1) CDRRMO serves as CDRRMC secretariat with documented secretariat procedures  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(17)\n- **A2.1** (M, weight 3) Organizational structure follows JMC 2014-1: Head + Administration & Training, Research & Planning, and Operations & Warning divisions  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC-DILG-DBM-CSC JMC 2014-1\n- **A2.2** (M, weight 3) CDRRMO Head is a permanent plantilla position filled by a qualified appointee  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(b); JMC 2014-1\n- **A2.3** (M, weight 3) Minimum plantilla positions per JMC 2014-1 created and filled (ratio of filled/required)  \n  *Basis:* JMC 2014-1; CSC QS\n- **A2.4** (M, weight 2) Job descriptions/position descriptions defined for every position, aligned to RA 10121 functions  \n  *Basis:* CSC rules; JMC 2014-1\n- **A2.7** (M, weight 2) Personnel welfare and safety: insurance, hazard pay, PPE, psychosocial support for responders  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12; RA 11036 (MHPSS); CSC rules\n- **A2.9** (M, weight 2) Anticipatory action focal person designated in the CDRRMO, with AA training for staff, BDRRMCs, ACDVs and partner CSOs (incl. community-based early warning and early action)  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 1; Rule VII','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('5ac43e3f-4099-51cb-b931-a68cfd65256a'::uuid,'77080547-c4e3-52f7-8908-02ce0fc78258'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.3 – DRRM and Contingency Planning\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A3.1** (M, weight 3) Local DRRM Plan (LDRRMP) current, approved by Sanggunian, aligned with NDRRMP 2020-2030 and Sendai Framework  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(6); NDRRMP 2020-2030\n- **A3.2** (M, weight 2) DRR-CCA mainstreamed in CDP, CLUP, ELA and LCCAP (hazard data used in land use and investment decisions)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 2; RA 9729 as amended by RA 10174; HLURB/DHSUD CLUP guidelines\n- **A3.3** (M, weight 3) Hazard-specific Contingency Plans for priority hazards (flood/flash flood, landslide, earthquake/tsunami, tropical cyclone, industrial/geothermal), updated and tested  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC CP guidelines; OCD CP Manual\n- **A3.4** (M, weight 2) Public Service Continuity Plan (PSCP) prepared covering CDRRMO and essential city services  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC/OCD PSCP Guidebook\n- **A3.7** (GP, weight 2) Barangay DRRM plans (85 barangays) reviewed/consolidated by CDRRMO  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(6); DILG MC on BDRRM\n- **A5.1** (M, weight 3) Updated hazard maps for all priority hazards (MGB, PHIVOLCS, PAGASA, GeoRiskPH/HazardHunterPH) at barangay scale  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(3); NDRRMP\n- **A5.2** (M, weight 3) Climate & Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)/ CRA completed with exposure (population, buildings, critical facilities) and vulnerability data  \n  *Basis:* DHSUD/CCC CDRA guidelines; RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(3)\n- **A5.3** (M, weight 2) Disaggregated population database (sex, age, disability, IP, pregnant/lactating) for at-risk households  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(5); RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 10821\n- **A5.8** (GP, weight 2) Critical facilities list (EOC, hospitals, evacuation centers, water, power, telecom, bridges, port) with dependencies mapped as potential failure chains and used to set protection and restoration priorities  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 2.3.1; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule I Sec. 5(b),(d)\n- **A7.6** (M, weight 2) Inclusive DRRM: GAD, PWD, senior citizen, children, IP considerations embedded in plans and services, with vulnerable-sector representatives confirming they were engaged  \n  *Basis:* RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 1(a); UNDRR DRSC 7.2.2\n- **C1.1** (GP, weight 2) Most-probable and most-severe risk scenarios inform CDP/CLUP priorities and the location of major investments  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 1.1.1; RA 10121 Sec. 2; DHSUD CLUP guidelines\n- **C1.2** (GP, weight 1) DRR/CCA screening step in project and budget approval (LDC, LFC)  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 1.3.1; climate change expenditure tagging guidelines\n- **C1.3** (GP, weight 1) CDRA estimates losses beyond physical assets: livelihoods and jobs, housing and displacement, agriculture, ecosystems, cultural heritage  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 2.2.2; DHSUD/CCC CDRA guidelines\n- **C1.4** (GP, weight 2) Risk-sensitive development guidelines applied in zoning and building permits, based on hazard maps  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 2.4.1; CLUP/ZO\n- **C3.1** (GP, weight 2) Critical protective ecosystems (upper Anilao-Malbasag watershed, upland forest cover, coastal mangroves) identified and monitored as risk-reduction assets  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 5.1.1; RA 9729 as amended','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('1008c58c-bb8c-5cab-82ec-2e441910ae6b'::uuid,'eafa0947-d859-57e8-b93e-affd8500f0a5'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.4 – Operations, Warning and Response\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A2.5** (M, weight 3) 24/7 operations capability with documented shift roster (Operations Center/Duty officers)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(16); EOC guidelines\n- **A2.7** (M, weight 2) Personnel welfare and safety: insurance, hazard pay, PPE, psychosocial support for responders  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12; RA 11036 (MHPSS); CSC rules\n- **A3.6** (M, weight 3) Evacuation plan with mapped routes, safe zones, centers and pre-emptive evacuation triggers per barangay  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; RA 12076; DILG Operation L!sto\n- **A6.1** (M, weight 3) Hazard-specific EWS operational for flood (incl. Anilao/Malbasag river systems), rain-induced landslide, tsunami (Ormoc Bay) and tropical cyclone  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(8); PAGASA/PHIVOLCS protocols\n- **A6.3** (M, weight 3) Last-mile dissemination channels tested to barangay/purok level (sirens, radio, SMS/ECBS, social media, barangay criers)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10639 (Free Mobile Disaster Alerts); NDRRMC EWS\n- **A6.4** (GP, weight 2) Warnings issued in local language (Cebuano/ Ormocanon) and accessible formats (sign language, visual alerts for PWD)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; RA 11106 (FSL Act); RA 7277\n- **A6.7** (M, weight 2) Templates and channels for public notice of a State of Imminent Disaster and of the anticipatory actions required, incl. sector-specific impact-based advisories and reach to geographically isolated barangays  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 2\n- **A7.4** (M, weight 3) Permanent evacuation center(s) planned/established and existing ECs meet minimum standards (WASH, privacy, PWD access, child-friendly space)  \n  *Basis:* RA 12076 (Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act); RA 10821; DSWD standards\n- **A7.6** (M, weight 2) Inclusive DRRM: GAD, PWD, senior citizen, children, IP considerations embedded in plans and services, with vulnerable-sector representatives confirming they were engaged  \n  *Basis:* RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 1(a); UNDRR DRSC 7.2.2\n- **A7.9** (GP, weight 2) BDRRMCs can account for residents of affected puroks within 12 hours of impact (welfare check), shown in a drill or a real event  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 7.2.1\n- **A8.1** (M, weight 3) Incident Command System institutionalized: EO on ICS, trained IMT roster, pre-designated Incident Commanders  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC MC No. 4, s. 2012 (ICS)\n- **A8.2** (M, weight 3) Organized and trained response teams: SAR (WASAR/CSSR/HAZMAT as relevant), EMS/ambulance, fire coordination with BFP  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(16); RA 11589 (BFP)\n- **A8.3** (M, weight 2) Response equipment and vehicles inventoried, serviceable and maintained (preventive maintenance schedule)  \n  *Basis:* COA property rules; RA 10121\n- **A8.7** (M, weight 2) Management of the Dead and Missing (MDM) and Rapid Damage Assessment & Needs Analysis (RDANA) teams designated and trained  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC MDM & RDANA guidelines\n- **A8.8** (M, weight 3) Anticipatory action protocols by hazard and lead time: office-order templates for prepositioning (relief, medical supplies, animal feed, water and power for evacuation centers), deployment of heavy equipment, transport and personnel, pre-emptive evacuation incl. livestock and pets, CCCM, and safeguarding of critical infrastructure; aligned with CPs, Oplan L!sto and evacuation plans  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Secs. 1, 3, 4, 6\n- **A8.9** (M, weight 2) Anticipatory social amelioration: families identified and prioritized using CBMS or the prevailing registry; data-sharing agreements compliant with RA 10173; local guidelines for anticipatory cash or vouchers issued with the CSWDO  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 7; RA 11315 (CBMS); RA 10173\n- **C2.1** (GP, weight 3) Structural safety of the EOC, evacuation centers and critical public facilities assessed by the Building Official, with a rapid safety inspection procedure before forecast impact  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 4.3.1, 4.4.2; PD 1096; RA 12076; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 4(c)(i)','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('6cc001df-e80d-5b30-a27d-30dc304285a3'::uuid,'d7190e43-31f8-55d7-b646-04574073dedf'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.5 – DRRM Financing\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A3.5** (M, weight 3) Annual LDRRMF Investment Plan (LDRRMFIP) prepared, approved and consistent with LDRRMP  \n  *Basis:* JMC 2013-1\n- **A4.1** (M, weight 3) LDRRMF set at not less than 5% of estimated revenue from regular sources  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 21; JMC 2013-1\n- **A4.2** (M, weight 3) 70% Mitigation/Preparedness and 30% Quick Response Fund (QRF) allocation observed  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 21; JMC 2013-1\n- **A4.3** (M, weight 2) Unexpended LDRRMF transferred to Special Trust Fund and used within 5 years  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 21; COA Circular 2012-002\n- **A4.4** (GP, weight 2) LDRRMF utilization rate monitored and reported (target >= 80% of current-year 70% fund)  \n  *Basis:* JMC 2013-1; COA AAR findings\n- **A4.5** (M, weight 2) Emergency procurement procedures (negotiated procurement - emergency cases) documented and pre-arranged (framework agreements, pre-qualified suppliers)  \n  *Basis:* RA 12009 (New Government Procurement Act) & IRR\n- **A4.6** (M, weight 2) Anticipatory action funding pathway: LDRRMFIP sets an AA line within the cap of 10% of the 70% LDRRMF; other sources mapped (Special Trust Fund balances, donations, ODA); Budget, Accounting and BAC procedures allow immediate disbursement and observe the 30%/70% tranche rule around the 3-day lead time  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule V Secs. 1-3; RA 10121 Sec. 21; RA 12009\n- **A4.7** (GP, weight 1) Risk transfer/financing instruments explored (indemnity insurance of public assets, PSF access, partnerships)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMP; RA 10174 (People''s Survival Fund); DOF-BTr NIP\n- **A4.8** (M, weight 2) Clean COA findings on LDRRMF in last audit year (or findings addressed)  \n  *Basis:* COA Circular 2012-002\n- **A4.9** (GP, weight 2) QRF and LDRRMF adequacy tested against the most severe credible scenario (e.g., a Thelma-type flash flood or Yolanda-strength typhoon), with the shortfall and other fund sources identified  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 3.2.3, 3.2.4; JMC 2013-1\n- **A10.5** (M, weight 1) Full Disclosure Policy: LDRRMF and utilization posted publicly  \n  *Basis:* DILG FDP MCs; RA 10121 Sec. 21\n- **C1.2** (GP, weight 1) DRR/CCA screening step in project and budget approval (LDC, LFC)  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 1.3.1; climate change expenditure tagging guidelines','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('ea2c5737-7918-5543-ac50-e7b037af5d62'::uuid,'d61fc0e9-7136-5375-ab68-40132bc8dd3c'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.6 – Capacity Development\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A2.6** (GP, weight 2) Training needs analysis done and competency-based training plan for staff (ICS, BLS/SFA, WASAR, CSSR, MOSAR, EOC, PDNA, GIS)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 14; OCD/DRRM Training Institute curricula\n- **A2.8** (GP, weight 1) Volunteer/auxiliary responder management system (accreditation, registry, deployment, insurance), incl. mobilization of ACDVs for anticipatory action  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 13; NDRRMC volunteer guidelines; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 5\n- **A2.9** (M, weight 2) Anticipatory action focal person designated in the CDRRMO, with AA training for staff, BDRRMCs, ACDVs and partner CSOs (incl. community-based early warning and early action)  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 1; Rule VII\n- **A2.10** (GP, weight 1) Inventory of DRRM-relevant skills across the CDRRMO, partner offices and barangays (SAR, engineering, logistics, GIS, PDNA, MHPSS) updated within the last 12 months  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 6.1.1\n- **A6.6** (GP, weight 1) IEC program on hazards and preparedness with annual reach targets  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(13)\n- **A7.1** (M, weight 2) Annual training program for city personnel, BDRRMCs, schools and communities implemented  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(12), Sec. 14\n- **A7.2** (M, weight 2) Participation in quarterly Nationwide Simultaneous Earthquake Drill (NSED) and hazard-specific drills with evaluation  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC NSED guidelines\n- **A7.3** (GP, weight 2) BDRRMC functionality across 85 barangays assessed (e.g., Listo si KAP) with support plan for low-functioning barangays  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; DILG MC 2025-035 (Listo si KAP)\n- **A7.7** (GP, weight 1) Schools, hospitals and critical facilities included in preparedness planning and drills  \n  *Basis:* DepEd/DOH DRRM policies; Safe Schools/Hospitals\n- **A7.8** (GP, weight 1) Refresher cycle (at least annual) set for trained responders, EOC staff and BDRRMC members, with attendance tracked  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 6.4.2','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('99af31ad-0c1a-52c6-970b-815d9b7fc0ff'::uuid,'aec20d50-301b-57ce-87d4-64154502a0ef'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.7 – Knowledge Management, Reporting and M&E\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A5.4** (GP, weight 2) DRRM information system/ database with disaster event history, damage and losses (incl. 1991 flood, 2013 Yolanda, 2017 earthquake records)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(5); Sendai monitoring\n- **A5.5** (M, weight 2) Data privacy compliance for beneficiary and affected-population data (DPO, consent, data sharing agreements)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act); NPC circulars\n- **A5.6** (GP, weight 1) GIS capability (software, trained staff, updated base maps) within Research & Planning  \n  *Basis:* JMC 2014-1 (R&P functions)\n- **A5.7** (GP, weight 2) One maintained, shared source of risk and readiness information (hazard maps, plans, contacts, readiness summary) open to CDRRMC member offices, with a public summary posted  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 1.4.1, 6.3.1, 6.3.2; RA 10173\n- **A5.9** (GP, weight 1) Agreed update cycle: hazard data every 3 years or less; exposure, vulnerability and critical-facility data every 18 months or less  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 2.5.1\n- **A10.3** (M, weight 2) Regular reporting to OCD Region VIII/RDRRMC and DILG (SitReps, LDRRMF reports, Operation L!sto)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; DILG MCs\n- **A10.4** (M, weight 2) Monitoring & evaluation framework for LDRRMP with indicators and annual review  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(6)\n- **A10.6** (GP, weight 2) After-action reviews conducted after significant events and lessons integrated into plans  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC; good practice\n- **A10.7** (GP, weight 1) Participation in performance recognition/assessment systems (Gawad KALASAG Seal, SGLG Disaster Preparedness) and gaps acted upon  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC GKS guidelines; RA 11292 (SGLG Act)\n- **A10.8** (M, weight 2) Post-declaration report within 45 days of lifting a State of Imminent Disaster (declaration status, AA fund utilization, estimated economic and social costs minimized); reporting route confirmed with OCD VIII  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule VI Sec. 1\n- **A10.9** (GP, weight 1) Structured learning from other LGUs and networks (exchanges, OCD forums, MCR2030), with resulting changes documented  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 6.6.1','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('955f1841-625f-5638-a229-7b93c39869e2'::uuid,'cb5fb5fd-5044-57b7-84fe-dff921b0c759'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.8 – Rehabilitation and Recovery\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A9.1** (GP, weight 2) Post-Disaster Needs Assessment (PDNA) capacity: trained team and templates ready  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC PDNA guidelines; OCD\n- **A9.2** (M, weight 2) Rehabilitation & recovery plan prepared after major events, with build-back-better and resettlement provisions  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(11); NDRRMP Pillar 4\n- **A9.3** (GP, weight 1) Recovery data (damage, losses, livelihood, housing) tracked to completion  \n  *Basis:* Sendai Framework targets C-D\n- **A9.4** (GP, weight 1) Psychosocial and livelihood recovery services coordinated with CSWDO, CHO, PESO  \n  *Basis:* RA 11036; DSWD protocols\n- **A9.5** (GP, weight 2) Pre-disaster recovery framework: roles, PDNA activation, temporary shelter, debris management and recovery fund sources agreed before an event  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC Essential 10; NDRRMP Pillar 4','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('dfa35a60-bd59-5835-9d07-862e7c5b28ef'::uuid,'57e7eec1-e655-50fd-8bc4-e7b956b654fc'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Ch.9 – Partnerships\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A10.1** (GP, weight 2) MOAs/ mutual aid agreements with neighbouring LGUs (Kananga, Albuera, Merida, Isabel etc.) and Province of Leyte  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(15); RA 7160 Sec. 33\n- **A10.2** (GP, weight 1) Partnerships with CSOs, private sector (incl. energy/geothermal operators), academe and faith-based groups documented, with pre- and post-event roles defined and at least annual coordination meetings  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(15); UNDRR DRSC 6.1.2, 6.1.4\n- **C2.2** (GP, weight 2) Pre-crisis and priority-restoration arrangements with power, water and telecom providers (load-shedding and water-rationing plans, cell-on-wheels, restoration priority list)  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC Essential 8; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 4(c)(ii)-(iv)\n- **C3.2** (GP, weight 1) Arrangements with neighbouring LGUs and DENR where watersheds or protective ecosystems extend beyond city limits  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 5.3.1, 5.3.2; RA 7160 Sec. 33\n- **C5.1** (GP, weight 1) Business continuity outreach to local businesses based on shared hazard scenarios (outreach only; no mandate implied)  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 7.3.2','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('8c4febb5-60d9-575e-a8ef-113012d3083d'::uuid,'154dc4f3-6d53-5aec-82d7-474fc6d004b1'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.1 – Legal Basis\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **B1.1** (M, weight 3) EOC established by EO/ordinance with defined mandate, authority and relationship to CDRRMC and ICS  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC MC No. 131, s. 2018 (EOC Guidelines); RA 10121\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('098330dc-3302-5bf2-92e7-2e41e9d2ae8e'::uuid,'33665e28-8c73-5cda-912b-4ddcf179ea16'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.2 – Concept of Operations and Activation\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A1.8** (M, weight 3) State of Imminent Disaster procedure: CDRRMC coordinates with RDRRMC VIII when the pre-disaster risk assessment shows reasonable certainty of severe impact; request format to RDRRMC (24-hour reply); Mayor''s EO template; adoption rule when the Region (or Province, applicability to an ICC to be confirmed) is declared; lifting grounds and authority  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule II Sec. 6; Rule III Secs. 2-4\n- **A6.2** (M, weight 3) Warning thresholds linked to specific actions (alert levels -> pre-emptive evacuation, class/work suspension, State of Imminent Disaster request) in written protocols, built to take up NDRRMC impact-based triggers once issued  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; RA 12287; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule II Sec. 2; DILG Operation L!sto\n- **A8.6** (M, weight 3) Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment (PDRA) conducted before forecast hazards, with anticipatory actions recorded  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC PDRA protocol; RA 12287; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule II Sec. 5\n- **A8.8** (M, weight 3) Anticipatory action protocols by hazard and lead time: office-order templates for prepositioning (relief, medical supplies, animal feed, water and power for evacuation centers), deployment of heavy equipment, transport and personnel, pre-emptive evacuation incl. livestock and pets, CCCM, and safeguarding of critical infrastructure; aligned with CPs, Oplan L!sto and evacuation plans  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Secs. 1, 3, 4, 6\n- **B1.2** (M, weight 3) Alert/activation levels defined (e.g., White-normal, Blue-alert, Red-full activation) with hazard-based triggers and a defined EOC posture when a State of Imminent Disaster is declared  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines; NDRRMC alert protocol; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 1\n- **B1.3** (M, weight 3) Activation, escalation, de-escalation and deactivation procedures with designated authority  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B1.4** (M, weight 2) Concept of operations documents EOC functions: coordination, information management, resource management, policy support  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B1.5** (M, weight 2) EOC linkage to Provincial (Leyte) and Regional (OCD VIII) EOCs defined  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **C3.3** (GP, weight 2) Environmental anticipatory actions: declogging of waterways and drainage, slope stabilization, safe debris and carcass disposal  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 10(d)','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('bb994eca-e94d-50ac-9768-86d6527a9735'::uuid,'81fe614e-15e5-516a-9f84-8f033aec5526'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.3 – Facility, Site and Continuity\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A3.3** (M, weight 3) Hazard-specific Contingency Plans for priority hazards (flood/flash flood, landslide, earthquake/tsunami, tropical cyclone, industrial/geothermal), updated and tested  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC CP guidelines; OCD CP Manual\n- **A3.6** (M, weight 3) Evacuation plan with mapped routes, safe zones, centers and pre-emptive evacuation triggers per barangay  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; RA 12076; DILG Operation L!sto\n- **A5.8** (GP, weight 2) Critical facilities list (EOC, hospitals, evacuation centers, water, power, telecom, bridges, port) with dependencies mapped as potential failure chains and used to set protection and restoration priorities  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 2.3.1; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule I Sec. 5(b),(d)\n- **A6.1** (M, weight 3) Hazard-specific EWS operational for flood (incl. Anilao/Malbasag river systems), rain-induced landslide, tsunami (Ormoc Bay) and tropical cyclone  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(8); PAGASA/PHIVOLCS protocols\n- **B2.1** (M, weight 3) EOC site located outside high flood, landslide, tsunami and liquefaction zones (checked vs hazard maps)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines; hazard maps\n- **B2.2** (M, weight 3) Building structural integrity assessed (seismic - Philippine Fault proximity) and certified  \n  *Basis:* NBC (PD 1096); NSCP; DPWH\n- **B2.3** (M, weight 3) Backup power (generator + fuel for >=72 hours, UPS) tested regularly  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines; good practice\n- **B2.4** (GP, weight 2) Functional layout: operations floor, briefing/decision room, comms room, rest area, sanitation, kitchen  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B2.5** (GP, weight 1) Water, food and sanitation supplies for EOC staff for >=72 hours  \n  *Basis:* Good practice; PSCP\n- **B2.6** (GP, weight 1) Physical security and access control (ID, visitor log, CCTV)  \n  *Basis:* Good practice\n- **B2.7** (M, weight 2) Designated and equipped alternate EOC with relocation procedure  \n  *Basis:* PSCP Guidebook; NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **C2.1** (GP, weight 3) Structural safety of the EOC, evacuation centers and critical public facilities assessed by the Building Official, with a rapid safety inspection procedure before forecast impact  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 4.3.1, 4.4.2; PD 1096; RA 12076; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 4(c)(i)','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('59b61309-18d9-583e-b87b-4e369fd494bc'::uuid,'613139e9-8daa-5fb3-b4a4-a8a8dc45b04c'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.4 – Communications and Sector Coordination\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A2.5** (M, weight 3) 24/7 operations capability with documented shift roster (Operations Center/Duty officers)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(16); EOC guidelines\n- **A8.4** (GP, weight 2) Emergency hotline integrated with E911 and dispatch protocol (call-taking to dispatch time tracked)  \n  *Basis:* EO 56 s. 2018 (E911); DILG guidelines\n- **A8.5** (M, weight 2) Response clusters organized at city level mirroring national cluster approach (Food & NFI, Health, WASH, CCCM, Logistics, Search Rescue & Retrieval, Law & Order, Education, Protection, ETC, MDM)  \n  *Basis:* NDCC MC No. 12, s. 2008; NDRRM Plan Response pillar\n- **B3.1** (M, weight 3) Redundant communications: VHF/UHF radio, HF, satellite phone, landline, mobile, internet (min. 3 independent channels)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines; NTC frequency assignment\n- **B3.2** (M, weight 2) Radio network covering all 85 barangays and responders, with call signs and net-control procedure  \n  *Basis:* NTC licensing; ETC cluster\n- **B3.3** (GP, weight 2) Backup internet (secondary ISP/satellite) and continuity of digital systems  \n  *Basis:* Good practice; DICT\n- **B3.4** (M, weight 2) Communications plan/ directory (agencies, barangays, clusters) updated within last 6 months  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B3.5** (GP, weight 1) Cybersecurity and information security measures for EOC systems  \n  *Basis:* RA 10173; DICT National Cybersecurity Plan\n- **B3.6** (GP, weight 1) Display and monitoring equipment (video wall/monitors, weather feeds, CCTV feeds)  \n  *Basis:* Good practice\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **C4.1** (GP, weight 3) Health anticipatory action: Health Cluster activation, facility service-continuity and surge plans, prepositioned commodities, health emergency response teams on standby  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 9\n- **C4.2** (GP, weight 2) Education anticipatory action: schools'' AA plans and learning-continuity modalities  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 10(a); RA 10821\n- **C4.3** (GP, weight 2) Agri-fisheries anticipatory action using RSBSA, FishR and livestock registries; early action guidelines on storage, inputs and animal health  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 8\n- **C4.4** (GP, weight 1) Pre-impact price monitoring and anti-profiteering of basic goods  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 10(c); RA 7581 (Price Act)','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('4b32eccb-7345-5f36-9f7d-69995cc9217c'::uuid,'9cd07e9f-bfa7-543a-9c6e-f56c433380de'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.5 – Organization, Staffing and Public Information\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A5.1** (M, weight 3) Updated hazard maps for all priority hazards (MGB, PHIVOLCS, PAGASA, GeoRiskPH/HazardHunterPH) at barangay scale  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(3); NDRRMP\n- **A5.3** (M, weight 2) Disaggregated population database (sex, age, disability, IP, pregnant/lactating) for at-risk households  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(5); RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 10821\n- **A6.5** (GP, weight 1) Designated public information officer and risk communication plan (incl. rumor/misinformation management)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC public info protocols\n- **A6.7** (M, weight 2) Templates and channels for public notice of a State of Imminent Disaster and of the anticipatory actions required, incl. sector-specific impact-based advisories and reach to geographically isolated barangays  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 2\n- **A10.3** (M, weight 2) Regular reporting to OCD Region VIII/RDRRMC and DILG (SitReps, LDRRMF reports, Operation L!sto)  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; DILG MCs\n- **A10.8** (M, weight 2) Post-declaration report within 45 days of lifting a State of Imminent Disaster (declaration status, AA fund utilization, estimated economic and social costs minimized); reporting route confirmed with OCD VIII  \n  *Basis:* RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule VI Sec. 1\n- **B4.1** (M, weight 3) EOC organizational structure defined (EOC Manager, Operations, Planning/Information, Logistics, Admin/Finance, liaison desks)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines; ICS\n- **B4.2** (M, weight 3) Pre-designated EOC staff with alternates (at least 2 deep per key position)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B4.3** (M, weight 2) Duty roster for sustained 24/7 operations with shift handover protocol  \n  *Basis:* Good practice\n- **B4.4** (M, weight 2) Agency/cluster liaison desks identified (CSWDO, CHO, CEO, PNP, BFP, AFP, DepEd, utilities, Red Cross)  \n  *Basis:* NDCC MC 12 s. 2008; EOC Guidelines\n- **B4.5** (GP, weight 2) Position checklists/job action sheets for each EOC role  \n  *Basis:* Good practice\n- **B4.6** (GP, weight 1) Responder welfare in EOC: rest cycles, meals, MHPSS, family support  \n  *Basis:* RA 11036; good practice\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('f7ab1636-e4a2-5bd3-b710-eb5b1d5d1a22'::uuid,'a0cad148-d5ee-5805-bf81-4b86fbf1aeac'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.6 – Information Management and Reporting\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A4.5** (M, weight 2) Emergency procurement procedures (negotiated procurement - emergency cases) documented and pre-arranged (framework agreements, pre-qualified suppliers)  \n  *Basis:* RA 12009 (New Government Procurement Act) & IRR\n- **A7.4** (M, weight 3) Permanent evacuation center(s) planned/established and existing ECs meet minimum standards (WASH, privacy, PWD access, child-friendly space)  \n  *Basis:* RA 12076 (Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act); RA 10821; DSWD standards\n- **A7.5** (M, weight 2) Prepositioned relief stocks/food packs and non-food items with inventory and replenishment triggers  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; DSWD-LGU protocols; Operation L!sto\n- **A7.9** (GP, weight 2) BDRRMCs can account for residents of affected puroks within 12 hours of impact (welfare check), shown in a drill or a real event  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 7.2.1\n- **A8.3** (M, weight 2) Response equipment and vehicles inventoried, serviceable and maintained (preventive maintenance schedule)  \n  *Basis:* COA property rules; RA 10121\n- **A10.1** (GP, weight 2) MOAs/ mutual aid agreements with neighbouring LGUs (Kananga, Albuera, Merida, Isabel etc.) and Province of Leyte  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121 Sec. 12(c)(15); RA 7160 Sec. 33\n- **B5.1** (M, weight 3) Standard SitRep/ Report templates aligned with NDRRMC/OCD formats and fixed reporting schedule  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC reporting protocol\n- **B5.2** (M, weight 3) Barangay-to-city reporting flow (BDRRMC -> EOC) with defined timelines and formats  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; DILG\n- **B5.3** (GP, weight 2) Common Operating Picture maintained (dashboard/ GIS map of incidents, evacuees, resources)  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B5.4** (M, weight 2) Evacuation center and affected-family tracking (DAFAC/ registration) feeds EOC data  \n  *Basis:* DSWD DAFAC; RA 10821\n- **B5.5** (M, weight 2) EOC logbook/ significant events log and document retention  \n  *Basis:* Good practice; NAP rules\n- **B5.6** (M, weight 2) Weather/hazard monitoring routine (PAGASA, PHIVOLCS feeds) with bulletins logged and disseminated  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; PAGASA protocols\n- **B5.7** (GP, weight 1) Inbound citizen reporting (hotline, SMS, social media) feeds the common operating picture after a verification step  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 7.4.2\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('6f2e209c-33ef-5ac6-bcc3-99c5096980b3'::uuid,'f3002288-2f46-522f-911e-0fc6aa0b6afe'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.7 – Coordination and Resource Management\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A3.4** (M, weight 2) Public Service Continuity Plan (PSCP) prepared covering CDRRMO and essential city services  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC/OCD PSCP Guidebook\n- **B2.7** (M, weight 2) Designated and equipped alternate EOC with relocation procedure  \n  *Basis:* PSCP Guidebook; NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B6.1** (M, weight 3) ICS-EOC interface defined: EOC supports Incident Commander(s); no duplication of command  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC MC No. 4 s. 2012; EOC Guidelines\n- **B6.2** (M, weight 3) Resource management system: request, tracking, mobilization and demobilization of resources  \n  *Basis:* ICS resource management; EOC Guidelines\n- **B6.3** (M, weight 2) Resource inventory (city, barangays, partners, private sector) accessible from EOC  \n  *Basis:* RA 10121; ICS\n- **B6.4** (GP, weight 1) Procedures for receiving and managing external assistance/ donations and volunteers  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC guidelines on international/ external assistance\n- **B6.5** (GP, weight 2) Decision-support briefings to CDRRMC/Mayor at fixed intervals during activation  \n  *Basis:* EOC Guidelines\n- **B6.6** (GP, weight 2) Pre-arranged resource commitments from partners and suppliers set out by item, quantity, activation trigger and contact in signed agreements (incl. supplier agreements tapped for anticipatory action)  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC 1.2.4; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 6(a)(ii)\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **C2.2** (GP, weight 2) Pre-crisis and priority-restoration arrangements with power, water and telecom providers (load-shedding and water-rationing plans, cell-on-wheels, restoration priority list)  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC Essential 8; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 4(c)(ii)-(iv)\n- **C2.3** (GP, weight 2) Transport anticipatory actions: pre-inspection of bridges, alternate routes and traffic teams, coordination with seaport and terminal operators  \n  *Basis:* UNDRR DRSC Essential 8; RA 12287 IRR (2026) Rule IV Sec. 10(b)','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('890fcbd5-6c8a-5e82-b573-37f10db30d85'::uuid,'b565a903-0418-5524-9c0d-3839f503cacd'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.8 – Training and Exercises\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B7.2** (M, weight 2) EOC staff trained (EOC operations course, ICS, IM) with records  \n  *Basis:* OCD/DRRM training; NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B7.3** (M, weight 2) Annual exercise program (tabletop, functional, full-scale) testing EOC activation  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC; HSEEP-style good practice\n- **B7.4** (GP, weight 2) After-action review after each activation with improvement plan tracked  \n  *Basis:* Good practice','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('723c1671-a5c8-598b-b523-75fcdf5873e5'::uuid,'db682ca2-878b-59f5-9751-3d1a1d8dae79'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# EOC Ch.9 – Sustainability\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **B7.1** (M, weight 3) Written EOC SOPs/ Manual approved and disseminated to all EOC staff  \n  *Basis:* NDRRMC EOC Guidelines\n- **B7.5** (M, weight 2) Dedicated EOC budget for operations, maintenance and upgrades in LDRRMFIP  \n  *Basis:* JMC 2013-1\n- **B7.6** (GP, weight 1) Equipment maintenance schedule and annual EOC readiness self-check  \n  *Basis:* Good practice; PSCP','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft'),
('706d3315-068d-575b-814c-23e4e18a7f7e'::uuid,'4b930207-f0f5-5106-b657-d5057ebf28ad'::uuid,'v0.1 skeleton',E'# MOO Annex JDs – Job descriptions\n\n> Skeleton generated from IAT v1.1. Replace each requirement with the City''s procedure, standard or form.\n\n## Requirements this section must meet\n\n- **A2.4** (M, weight 2) Job descriptions/position descriptions defined for every position, aligned to RA 10121 functions  \n  *Basis:* CSC rules; JMC 2014-1','Generated from IAT v1.1','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('9b761332-d589-50e9-b57e-91588af85b3b'::uuid,'c906fb73-66a3-5477-8732-349b15f6e5c4'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'> **Reviewer note added when this draft was loaded into the platform (31 Oct 2026).** Section 1.1 quotes the workbook Dashboard as last saved (Part A 2.38, Part B 1.66, Part C 1.29, combined 2.18, mandatory compliance 39.4%, 30 High gaps). Those cached values were never recalculated after the 30 Oct edits. Recalculating from the workbook''s own scores gives Part A 2.35, Part B 1.47, Part C 1.26, combined 2.04, mandatory compliance 34.2% and 37 High gaps. Under the IAT evidence rule (interviews alone cannot raise a score above 2), A1.4 and A1.7 also rest on FGD self-scores only. Update the figures and the A1 table after the workbook is re-scored and recalculated.\n\n# **CHAPTER 1**\n\n# **LEGAL MANDATE, DRRM GOVERNANCE AND INSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY**\n\nORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO) AND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)\n\n**Revised Working Draft**\n\nAssessment-based\n\nSubject to institutional and legal validation\n\n## **1. Introduction**\n\nDisaster risk reduction and management (DRRM) in Ormoc City requires an integrated institutional system that connects executive leadership, policy direction, technical management, operational coordination and community-level action. Its effectiveness depends not only on the existence of the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council (CDRRMC), the City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (CDRRMO) and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC), but also on the clarity of their respective mandates, decision-making authority, functional relationships, accountability arrangements and operational interfaces.\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, governance and institutional accountability framework for the City''s CDRRMO and EOC. It defines how the City Government organizes, directs, coordinates, monitors and continuously improves its DRRM functions across normal operations, preparedness, anticipatory action, emergency response and recovery.\n\nThe chapter adopts a systems-thinking perspective: disaster risk is understood as the consequence of interconnected hazards, exposure, vulnerability, institutional capacities and dependencies across social, economic, environmental and physical systems. Accordingly, the City''s DRRM governance arrangements must operate as an integrated system rather than as a collection of independent offices, committees and emergency procedures.\n\nThe chapter also applies Total Quality Management (TQM) principles to institutional operations. These include process ownership, evidence-based decision-making, stakeholder participation, standardization, performance measurement, corrective action and continuous improvement.\n\nThe institutional provisions are informed by the [<u>Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.3</u>](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1L47rIY58uwKWk75YyV7LDELpF5PoJknJ/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=108981648814069460632&rtpof=true&sd=true), particularly its nine assessment sheets.\n\nThe assessment is treated as a diagnostic baseline, not as a substitute for legal compliance verification or formal institutional approval.\n\n### **1.1 Assessment-Informed Governance Priorities**\n\nThe October 2026 assessment identifies a significant distinction between the maturity of the CDRRMO, the EOC and their institutional interface.\n\nInstitutional Maturity Index\n\n# **2.38/4**\n\nCDRRMO\n\nDeveloping\n\n# **1.66/4**\n\nEOC\n\nDeveloping\n\n# **1.29/4**\n\nInstitutional interface\n\nInitial\n\n# **2.18/4**\n\nCombined institutional maturity\n\nDeveloping\n\n# **39.4%**\n\nMandatory compliance rate\n\n39.4% of scored mandatory indicators in Parts A and B attained a rating of 3 or 4.\n\nThe results demonstrate that the CDRRMO has a stronger institutional foundation than the EOC, while the interface between the two remains the weakest component. This is a critical systems issue: the existence of functioning CDRRMO activities does not automatically translate into a coordinated, documented and reliable EOC operating system.\n\nThe dashboard identifies 30 high-priority gaps: 19 in the CDRRMO assessment and 11 in the EOC assessment. The separate Part C assessment also identifies institutional dependencies that require action by other City Government offices and external partners.\n\nFor this chapter, the most consequential findings are:\n\n| **Assessment reference** | **Finding**                                                                                                                    | **Governance implication**                                                     |\n|--------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A1.1 – Score 2           | No ordinance establishing the CDRRMO as a regular department with the prescribed organizational divisions.                     | The legal and organizational foundation requires institutional strengthening.  |\n| A1.2 – Score 2           | CDRRMC membership requires updating, including at least four accredited CSO representatives and private-sector representation. | Governance must be more inclusive and representative.                          |\n| A1.3 – Score 4           | Quarterly CDRRMC meetings, minutes and resolutions are established.                                                            | Preserve and strengthen an existing institutional good practice.               |\n| A1.4 – Score 4           | CDRRMO performs the CDRRMC Secretariat function.                                                                               | Formalize the existing practice through documented procedures.                 |\n| A1.5 – Score 3           | Local DRRM-related ordinances exist, but evacuation procedures and hazard-sensitive zoning require improvement.                | Strengthen the connection between policy, risk information and implementation. |\n| A1.6 – Score 3           | State of Calamity arrangements have been used, but a comprehensive written procedure is needed.                                | Standardize declaration and post-declaration governance.                       |\n| A1.7 – Score 4           | DRRM representation in the City Development Council and Local Finance Committee is established.                                | Use existing planning and financial governance mechanisms more systematically. |\n| A1.8 – Score 1           | No localized State of Imminent Disaster procedure, request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template was identified.        | Establish an anticipatory governance process under RA 12287.                   |\n| A2.1 – Score 2           | Existing CDRRMO sections are not fully aligned with JMC 2014-1.                                                                | Clarify the permanent institutional structure and functional responsibilities. |\n| B1.1 – Score 2           | No specific EOC issuance defining its mandate and relationship with the CDRRMC and ICS was sighted.                            | Establish the EOC''s legal and operational authority.                           |\n| B6.1 – Score 1           | The EOC–ICS interface and chain of command are unclear.                                                                        | Define the distinction between incident command and EOC coordination.          |\n\nThe assessment also identifies broader system dependencies involving City Planning, Engineering, Budget, Environment, Social Welfare, Health, Education, Utilities and other institutions. These are addressed through the interface arrangements established in this chapter and the succeeding operational modules.\n\n**Management implication**: The priority is not simply to create more committees or issue additional policies. It is to establish a coherent governance system in which each mandate has an accountable owner, each process has a defined workflow, each decision has a legitimate authority, and each institutional output can be verified.\n\n**Assessment interpretation**: The workbook''s Dashboard reports 2.18/4.00 as the combined Institutional Maturity Index. Part C is separately reported and is not included in that index. Unscored indicators and provisional desk assessments must not be interpreted as proof of compliance.\n\n### **1.2 Interpretation of institutional Maturity and Compliance**\n\nThe assessment methodology distinguishes institutional maturity from mandatory compliance. A domain may achieve a relatively high maturity index while remaining legally non-compliant because one or more mandatory requirements have not attained the required rating.\n\nThis distinction is particularly important for Ormoc:\n\n- CDRRMO Legal Mandate and DRRM Governance (A1) has a maturity index of 2.59/4.00, but only 50% mandatory compliance.\n\n- EOC Legal Basis and Concept of Operations (B1) has a maturity index of 1.85/4.00, but 0% mandatory compliance.\n\n- The overall mandatory compliance rate is 39.4%, notwithstanding the combined maturity index of 2.18.\n\nThe implication is that institutional improvements must address both capability and compliance. A procedure that is practiced informally cannot automatically be treated as an established institutional control. Similarly, the existence of an executive order, organizational chart or operational facility does not establish compliance unless the relevant requirements are documented, approved, implemented and supported by evidence.\n\nThe assessment uses the following evidence-based maturity logic:\n\n| **Rating** | **Maturity classification**  | **Institutional interpretation**                                                             |\n|------------|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0          | Absent                       | No established arrangement.                                                                  |\n| 1          | Initial / Ad hoc             | Practice depends on individuals and is not adequately documented.                            |\n| 2          | Developing                   | Arrangement exists but is incomplete, outdated, unapproved or inconsistently implemented.    |\n| 3          | Compliant / Established      | Applicable standards are met through approved documentation and demonstrated implementation. |\n| 4          | Advanced / Institutionalized | Compliance is sustained, tested, reviewed and improved through documented learning.          |\n\nFor purposes of this MOO, a procedure shall not be considered fully institutionalized merely because it appears in the Manual. Its implementation must be demonstrated through records, operational outputs, verification and periodic review.\n\n## **2. Purpose**\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal and institutional governance arrangements governing the CDRRMO, CDRRMC and EOC.\n\nIt aims to:\n\n1.  Establish a clear and legally grounded division of authority among the Local Chief Executive, CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n2.  Define the institutional responsibilities, decision rights and accountability of participating City Government offices.\n\n3.  Institutionalize transparent, inclusive and evidence-based DRRM governance.\n\n4.  Establish procedures for CDRRMC meetings, resolutions, recommendations and action monitoring.\n\n5.  Standardize the City''s procedures for State of Calamity and State of Imminent Disaster declarations.\n\n6.  Strengthen vertical and horizontal coordination among the City Government, barangays, national government agencies, neighbouring LGUs, CSOs, private-sector partners and humanitarian organizations.\n\n7.  Establish process ownership, responsibility assignments and decision-making controls using the RACI framework.\n\n8.  Embed risk-informed governance, quality assurance, performance monitoring and continuous improvement into institutional operations.\n\n## **3. Scope and Application**\n\nThis chapter applies to the following institutional actors:\n\n- Office of the City Mayor;\n\n- CDRRMC and its Secretariat;\n\n- CDRRMO Head and functional divisions;\n\n- EOC Manager and EOC functional sections;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod;\n\n- City Government departments, offices and units;\n\n- BDRRMCs in all 85 barangays;\n\n- National government agencies and regional DRRM institutions;\n\n- Accredited CSOs, ACDVs, private-sector organizations and other partners.\n\nIts provisions apply under three operating conditions:\n\n| **Operating condition**                       | **Institutional focus**                                                                                                                                | **Primary governance mechanism**                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Normal operations                             | Risk reduction, institutional planning, preparedness, capacity development, monitoring and continuity.                                                 | CDRRMC oversight and CDRRMO management.                                           |\n| Heightened preparedness / anticipatory action | Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment, warning-action decisions, resource readiness, anticipatory measures and possible State of Imminent Disaster declaration. | CDRRMC coordination, Mayor''s executive authority and CDRRMO technical leadership. |\n| Emergency operations                          | Incident coordination, situational awareness, resource mobilization, policy support and operational reporting.                                         | Activated EOC, Incident Management Team and designated response clusters.         |\n\nThe Manual applies to all hazards and emergencies within the City''s jurisdiction, including hydrometeorological, geological, climate-related, technological and human-induced hazards, subject to applicable institutional mandates.\n\nIt shall be read together with the City''s approved DRRM plans, contingency plans, Public Service Continuity Plan, climate and disaster risk assessment, development plans, land-use plans and other applicable local policies.\n\n## **4. Legal, Policy and Standards Framework**\n\nThe institutional arrangements shall be interpreted and implemented within the following hierarchy of legal and policy instruments.\n\n### **4.1 Philippine Legal and Regulatory Framework**\n\n| **Instrument**                                                  | **Relevant provisions**                                                                  | **Application to this chapter**                                                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Republic Act No. 10121 (2010)                                   | Sections 11–17 and 21                                                                    | Establishment and functions of CDRRMC/CDRRMO, emergency coordination, State of Calamity and LDRRMF governance.   |\n| IRR of RA 10121                                                 | Relevant provisions on local councils, offices, coordination and declarations            | Operationalization of statutory institutional responsibilities.                                                  |\n| RA 7160 (1991) – Local Government Code                          | Sections 16, 33, 76 and relevant provisions on local legislative and executive authority | Local autonomy, inter-LGU cooperation, organizational structure and local governance.                            |\n| RA 12287 (2025) – Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act | Sections 3–7 and applicable implementing provisions                                      | Anticipatory declaration, pre-disaster risk assessment and early action.                                         |\n| IRR of RA 12287 (2026)                                          | Rules II–VII                                                                             | Local declaration procedures, anticipatory action, funding, capacity development and post-declaration reporting. |\n| RA 9729 (2009), as amended by RA 10174                          | Climate change adaptation and financing provisions                                       | Integration of climate resilience into DRRM governance and planning.                                             |\n| RA 10173 (2012) – Data Privacy Act                              | Personal information processing and protection                                           | Protection of affected-population, beneficiary, responder and institutional information.                         |\n| RA 9710 (2009) – Magna Carta of Women                           | Gender-responsive governance                                                             | Gender-responsive and inclusive institutional arrangements.                                                      |\n| RA 7277, as amended                                             | Rights and accessibility of persons with disabilities                                    | Accessible governance, warning and emergency services.                                                           |\n| RA 9994 (2010)                                                  | Senior citizen protection                                                                | Inclusion of older persons in DRRM decision-making and service arrangements.                                     |\n| RA 10821 (2016)                                                 | Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act                                           | Child protection and child-sensitive emergency arrangements.                                                     |\n| RA 11036 (2018) – Mental Health Act                             | Mental health services and protection                                                    | Institutional and responder psychosocial support.                                                                |\n| RA 12076 (2024) – Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act                      | Standards for permanent evacuation centers                                               | Institutional coordination on evacuation infrastructure and facility compliance.                                 |\n| RA 12009 (2024) – New Government Procurement Act                | Government procurement and applicable emergency procurement procedures                   | Resource governance and procurement accountability.                                                              |\n\nThe Local Government Code and RA 10121 provide the foundation for the City''s organizational and disaster governance responsibilities. RA 12287 introduces an additional anticipatory governance mechanism that must be integrated into existing DRRM processes rather than treated as a separate emergency procedure.\n\n### **4.2 Administrative and Operational Guidelines**\n\nThe following administrative and operational instruments shall guide institutional implementation:\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                    | **Application**                                                                                                   |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| NDRRMC-DILG-DBM-CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                                | LDRRMO organizational structure, staffing and functions.                                                          |\n| NDRRMC-DBM-DILG JMC No. 2013-1                                                    | LDRRMF programming and utilization.                                                                               |\n| COA Circular No. 2012-002                                                         | Accounting and reporting of the LDRRMF and Special Trust Fund.                                                    |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012                                         | Institutionalization and use of the Incident Command System.                                                      |\n| NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 131, s. 2018, as identified in the assessment      | EOC establishment, operationalization and management; issuance details require confirmation with OCD Region VIII. |\n| NDCC Memorandum Circular No. 12, s. 2008                                          | Cluster-based disaster response coordination.                                                                     |\n| RDRRMC VIII Resolution No. 12-006 and DILG VIII memorandum dated 26 February 2025 | Harmonized pre-disaster, during-disaster, post-disaster and terminal reporting.                                   |\n| OCD/NDRRMC Public Service Continuity Planning Guidebook                           | Continuity of essential functions, business impact analysis, alternate arrangements and succession.               |\n| DILG Operation L!sto and applicable annual issuances                              | Preparedness and institutional readiness of LGUs and barangays.                                                   |\n\nJMC No. 2014-1 specifically recognizes the LDRRMO as a mandatory office and provides for the Head and three core functional areas: Administration and Training, Research and Planning, and Operations and Warning. It also permits appropriate augmentation of staffing and organizational arrangements, subject to the applicable requirements.\n\n### **4.3 International Frameworks and Management Standards**\n\nThe MOO shall also be guided by the following frameworks:\n\n| **Framework / Standard**                                                          | **Application to institutional governance**                                                                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030                            | Strengthening disaster risk governance, understanding risk, investing in resilience and enhancing preparedness and recovery. |\n| UNDRR Making Cities Resilient 2030 – Ten Essentials                               | Alignment with the City''s urban resilience governance and institutional performance.                                         |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities – Detailed Level Assessment (2017) | Diagnostic reference for institutional and city-wide resilience assessment.                                                  |\n| ISO 31000:2018 – Risk Management Guidelines                                       | Integrating risk identification, analysis, evaluation, treatment, monitoring and communication into governance.              |\n| ISO 9001:2015 – Quality Management Systems                                        | Process ownership, documented procedures, performance evaluation, corrective action and continual improvement.               |\n| ISO 22301:2019 – Business Continuity Management Systems                           | Continuity of essential services, organizational resilience and disruption management.                                       |\n| Sphere Handbook (2018)                                                            | Humanitarian quality and accountability principles for emergency assistance.                                                 |\n| CREST / systems-thinking approaches                                               | Understanding interdependencies, feedback loops, cascading impacts and system-level interventions.                           |\n\nThese frameworks are complementary rather than substitutes for Philippine laws and regulations. Where a standard or international framework is more demanding than the applicable statutory minimum, the City may adopt it as a good-practice benchmark, subject to its authority and resources.\n\n### **4.4 Policy Hierarchy and Conflict Resolution**\n\nIn implementing this chapter, the following order shall apply:\n\n1.  Philippine Constitution and applicable statutes;\n\n2.  Valid implementing rules and regulations;\n\n3.  National administrative and operational issuances;\n\n4.  Provincial and regional coordination requirements, where applicable;\n\n5.  City ordinances and valid executive issuances;\n\n6.  Approved plans, policies and institutional procedures;\n\n7.  MOO protocols, checklists and supporting forms.\n\nIn case of inconsistency, the higher-ranking legal or regulatory instrument shall prevail. The CDRRMO shall refer material legal ambiguities to the City Legal Office and seek technical clarification from OCD Region VIII or the appropriate national agency.\n\nThe MOO shall maintain a legal and policy register, with an identified custodian and review date for each applicable instrument.\n\n## **5. Systems-Thinking Framework for DRRM Governance**\n\n### **5.1 Institutional System Architecture**\n\nOrmoc City''s DRRM governance shall operate as a connected system comprising five interdependent layers:\n\n1.  **Executive governance**: Political leadership, statutory authority and executive decision-making.\n\n2.  **Policy and oversigh**t: CDRRMC deliberation, policy direction, recommendations and monitoring.\n\n3.  **Institutional management**: CDRRMO planning, technical management, administration and implementation.\n\n4.  **Operational coordination**: EOC, ICS, response clusters and incident management arrangements.\n\n5.  **Community and external interface:** BDRRMCs, CSOs, private-sector institutions, utilities, national agencies and neighbouring LGUs.\n\nIntegrated CDRRMO–EOC governance system\n\nLOCAL CHIEF EXECUTIVE\n\nExecutive leadership and statutory authority\n\nCDRRMC\n\nPolicy direction, coordination and oversight\n\nCDRRMO\n\nPermanent institutional management and technical implementation\n\nNormal Operations\n\nRisk management, planning, preparedness, training and monitoring\n\nActivated EOC\n\nIncident coordination, information, resources and decision support\n\nBARANGAYS, CITY OFFICES, ICS / IMT, RESPONSE CLUSTERS AND PARTNERS\n\nOperational information, resource coordination, community action and feedback\n\nConceptual relationship. This is not a substitute for the statutory chain of authority or the approved ICS organizational chart.\n\nThe architecture recognizes that information, authority and resources move through different pathways.\n\n- Executive authority flows through legally established government structures.\n\n- Technical information flows from monitoring institutions, technical offices and communities toward decision-makers.\n\n- Operational instructions flow through the approved command and coordination arrangements.\n\n- Resources flow through authorized mobilization and accountability mechanisms.\n\n- Feedback and performance information flow back into planning, policy and institutional improvement.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall ensure that these pathways are connected but not conflated.\n\n### **5.2 The CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Interface**\n\nThe assessment identifies the institutional interface as the weakest component, with a separate maturity index of 1.29/4.00.\n\nThis indicates that the problem is not limited to individual office capacity. It concerns the connections between institutional actors, their processes, information systems and decision-making arrangements.\n\nThe following operating distinctions shall therefore apply:\n\n| **Dimension**            | **CDRRMO**                                                                                     | **EOC**                                                                                   |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Institutional status     | Permanent LGU office                                                                           | Operational coordination mechanism established through appropriate local issuance         |\n| Operating condition      | Continuous normal operations                                                                   | Activated according to approved triggers and authority                                    |\n| Primary accountability   | CDRRMO Head                                                                                    | Designated EOC Manager                                                                    |\n| Main function            | DRRM governance, planning, prevention, mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery support | Incident coordination, information management, resource coordination and decision support |\n| Planning horizon         | Strategic, annual and multi-year                                                               | Incident-specific and operational-period based                                            |\n| Information              | Maintains institutional risk and readiness information                                         | Consolidates and validates current incident information                                   |\n| Relationship with ICS    | Provides institutional support and coordinates preparedness arrangements                       | Supports the Incident Commander and Incident Management Team                              |\n| Institutional continuity | Maintains permanent office functions                                                           | Operates under activation, deactivation and continuity arrangements                       |\n\nThe EOC shall not replace the CDRRMO. Nor shall it become an alternative command structure for field response.\n\nThe Incident Commander shall retain incident-level command authority under the applicable ICS arrangements. The EOC shall facilitate coordination, provide information, mobilize support and elevate decisions requiring executive action.\n\n### **5.3 Institutional Interfaces and System Dependencies**\n\nPart C of the assessment identifies 15 institutional interface indicators. These are deliberately excluded from the CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Maturity Index because the primary operational ownership rests with other offices or external institutions.\n\nThe distinction is important for accountability.\n\n| **Interface area**                                                  | **Primary institutional owner**                       | **CDRRMO''s role**                                                                |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk-informed CDP/CLUP priorities and scenarios                     | City Planning and Development Office (CPDO)           | Supply risk evidence and provide DRR/CCA technical advice.                       |\n| DRR/CCA screening of projects and budgets                           | CPDO and City Budget Office                           | Provide hazard and resilience screening inputs.                                  |\n| Damage, livelihood and ecosystem loss analysis                      | CPDO and relevant sector offices                      | Supply disaster risk and impact information.                                     |\n| Hazard-sensitive zoning and building permits                        | CPDO and Zoning Administrator                         | Provide technical hazard information and participate in review.                  |\n| EOC, evacuation center and critical-facility structural assessments | City Engineer / Building Official                     | Identify critical facilities and coordinate risk prioritization.                 |\n| Power, water and telecommunications continuity                      | Utility providers and relevant City offices           | Coordinate restoration priorities and continuity agreements.                     |\n| Transport anticipatory action                                       | City Engineer, traffic office, DPWH and port operator | Integrate transport risk information into emergency coordination.                |\n| Watershed and ecosystem protection                                  | CENRO and DENR                                        | Integrate ecosystem-related hazards and protective functions into DRRM planning. |\n| Health anticipatory action                                          | City Health Office and hospitals                      | Coordinate the Health Cluster and operational information.                       |\n| Education continuity                                                | DepEd Schools Division                                | Coordinate school preparedness and learning-continuity arrangements.             |\n| Agriculture and fisheries anticipatory action                       | City Agriculture Office and City Veterinary Office    | Coordinate hazard information and response integration.                          |\n| Pre-impact price monitoring                                         | DTI and City Market Office                            | Facilitate information sharing with the EOC.                                     |\n| Business continuity outreach                                        | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce                        | Facilitate risk communication and preparedness outreach.                         |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall not assume technical or statutory responsibilities assigned to these offices. Its role is to establish the coordination mechanisms, information exchange and performance monitoring necessary for integrated city-wide resilience.\n\nThis distinction is essential to avoid institutional duplication and unfunded mandates.\n\n## **6. Governance Structure and Institutional Mandates**\n\n### **6.1 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council**\n\nThe CDRRMC shall serve as the City''s principal DRRM policy-making, coordinating and oversight body under RA 10121.\n\nIts composition and functions shall conform to Section 11 of the Act and its IRR.\n\nThe City shall maintain an updated Executive Order identifying the membership, institutional representation, designated alternates and coordination responsibilities of the Council.\n\nThe assessment identifies the following governance requirements:\n\n- Updating the CDRRMC Executive Order;\n\n- Meeting statutory CSO representation requirements, including at least four accredited CSO representatives;\n\n- Including private-sector representation;\n\n- Maintaining institutional representation rather than relying solely on individual appointments; and\n\n- Reviewing the membership register annually.\n\nThe CDRRMC shall ensure representation from relevant City Government offices, national agencies, public safety institutions, CSOs, the private sector and other prescribed members.\n\nIts principal functions shall include:\n\n1.  Approving, monitoring and evaluating the Local DRRM Plan;\n\n2.  Integrating DRR and climate change adaptation into local development plans and budgets;\n\n3.  Providing strategic direction for DRRM programs and policies;\n\n4.  Recommending pre-emptive and forced evacuation where warranted;\n\n5.  Recommending a State of Calamity declaration to the appropriate local legislative authority;\n\n6.  Coordinating anticipatory action under RA 12287;\n\n7.  Mobilizing institutional and external resources;\n\n8.  Monitoring the use of DRRM funds and other dedicated resources; and\n\n9.  Reviewing institutional performance, assessment findings and corrective actions.\n\n### **6.2 City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office**\n\nThe CDRRMO shall function as the City''s permanent institutional and technical mechanism for DRRM.\n\nIn accordance with Section 12 of RA 10121, it shall be responsible for setting the direction, development, implementation and coordination of local DRRM programs.\n\nIts responsibilities include:\n\n- Risk assessment and local risk information management;\n\n- Local DRRM planning and contingency planning;\n\n- Multi-hazard early warning;\n\n- Training, orientation and knowledge management;\n\n- Resource and capability inventory;\n\n- Coordination with communities, CSOs and private-sector partners;\n\n- Emergency monitoring and mobilization;\n\n- Organization and supervision of emergency response teams and ACDVs;\n\n- Recovery coordination;\n\n- CDRRMC Secretariat functions; and\n\n- Preparation and reporting of DRRM plans, budgets and fund utilization.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall perform these responsibilities through its approved organizational structure and in coordination with other City Government offices.\n\n### **6.3 Emergency Operations Center**\n\nThe EOC shall provide the City''s centralized mechanism for operational coordination during emergencies.\n\nThe EOC shall:\n\n1.  Maintain situational awareness;\n\n2.  Consolidate, validate and disseminate incident information;\n\n3.  Support CDRRMC and executive decision-making;\n\n4.  Facilitate coordination among the Incident Management Team and response clusters;\n\n5.  Coordinate resource requests and mobilization;\n\n6.  Maintain incident and decision records;\n\n7.  Facilitate vertical coordination with provincial and regional DRRM institutions, as applicable; and\n\n8.  Support transition from response to recovery.\n\nThe EOC shall operate within the authority established by the Mayor and the approved institutional arrangements.\n\n### **6.4 Office of the City Mayor**\n\nThe City Mayor shall exercise the powers and responsibilities vested in the Local Chief Executive under RA 7160, RA 10121, RA 12287 and other applicable laws.\n\nThe Mayor shall:\n\n- Provide executive leadership and direction;\n\n- Chair the CDRRMC;\n\n- Authorize actions within statutory authority;\n\n- Direct the mobilization of City Government offices and resources;\n\n- Issue valid executive orders and administrative directives;\n\n- Exercise the relevant declaration authority under RA 12287;\n\n- Approve institutional arrangements within the scope of executive authority; and\n\n- Elevate matters requiring legislative or higher-level government action.\n\nThe Mayor''s executive authority shall remain distinct from the legislative authority of the Sangguniang Panlungsod.\n\n### **6.5 Sangguniang Panlungsod**\n\nThe Sangguniang Panlungsod shall exercise its legislative and oversight responsibilities in accordance with RA 7160 and applicable DRRM legislation.\n\nIts relevant functions include:\n\n- Enacting the ordinance establishing or strengthening the CDRRMO;\n\n- Approving the necessary organizational and budgetary measures;\n\n- Considering CDRRMC recommendations for a State of Calamity declaration;\n\n- Enacting relevant local DRRM ordinances;\n\n- Exercising oversight over local DRRM policies and resource allocation; and\n\n- Supporting the institutionalization of the City''s resilience priorities.\n\nLegal distinction: The declaration of a State of Calamity under Section 16 of RA 10121 is issued by the local sanggunian upon the recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on damage assessment and needs analysis. It is not simply a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\nlawphil.net+1\n\n## **7. Institutional Governance Processes and Workflows**\n\nThe MOO shall define governance as a series of connected, controlled and measurable processes. Each process shall have:\n\n- A defined trigger or initiating condition;\n\n- A designated process owner;\n\n- Responsible actors;\n\n- A legally authorized decision-maker;\n\n- Required inputs and evidence;\n\n- Defined outputs and records;\n\n- Escalation and exception procedures; and\n\n- Performance and compliance indicators.\n\nThis approach ensures that governance decisions can be traced from initiation to approval, implementation, reporting and review.\n\n### **7.1 Process 1: CDRRMC Governance and Policy Decision-making**\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that DRRM policies, plans, priorities and institutional decisions are deliberated, approved or recommended by the appropriate authority and translated into accountable actions.\n\n**Workflow GOV-01: CDRRMC Governance**\n\n1.  **Initiation and agenda development**  \n    > CDRRMO identifies policy issues, assessment findings, risk developments and matters requiring Council action.\n\n2.  **Technical preparation**  \n    > Concerned offices submit technical papers, evidence, financial implications and recommendations.\n\n3.  **Secretariat processing  \n    > **CDRRMO consolidates agenda items, issues meeting notices and circulates supporting documents.\n\n4.  **Council deliberation  \n    > **CDRRMC members review evidence, assess implications and deliberate on proposed actions.\n\n5.  **Decision and documentation**  \n    > The Council approves matters within its authority or adopts recommendations for submission to the appropriate approving authority.\n\n6.  **Implementation and assignment  \n    > **The Secretariat records decisions, assigns action owners and establishes target dates.\n\n7.  **Monitoring and feedback  \n    > **CDRRMO tracks implementation and reports unresolved actions at succeeding meetings.\n\n**Required Output**\n\nApproved minutes, resolutions or recommendations, decision register and action-monitoring report.\n\n**Operating Requirements**\n\n| **Control point**    | **Required standard**                                                                                                     |\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Meeting frequency    | At least once every three months and as necessary.                                                                        |\n| Agenda               | Include standing items on risk developments, LDRRMP implementation, funding, assessment gaps and institutional readiness. |\n| Supporting documents | Technical papers, risk information, financial implications and draft resolutions, as applicable.                          |\n| Documentation        | Minutes, attendance, decisions, dissenting views where material, assigned owners and deadlines.                           |\n| Follow-through       | Action register reviewed at every succeeding meeting.                                                                     |\n| Review               | Annual assessment of Council functionality and effectiveness.                                                             |\n\nThe assessment records a score of 4 for A1.3. This established practice should be retained and strengthened through systematic action tracking, particularly for the 30 high-priority institutional gaps.\n\n### **7.2 Process 2: CDRRMC Secretariat Management**\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that Council proceedings, decisions and institutional records are complete, traceable and accessible to authorized stakeholders.\n\n**Workflow GOV-02: Secretariat Operations**\n\n1.  Receive agenda requests and supporting documents.\n\n2.  Prepare agenda, meeting notice and document package.\n\n3.  Coordinate with the Chairperson on scheduling and meeting requirements.\n\n4.  Record attendance, proceedings, resolutions and decisions.\n\n5.  Prepare draft minutes and resolutions for review and approval.\n\n6.  Distribute approved records to authorized members.\n\n7.  Enter decisions into the action register and assign responsible offices.\n\n8.  Track implementation and preserve the official records.\n\nRequired records: Meeting calendar, agenda, attendance, minutes, resolutions, action register and document distribution log.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall develop a Secretariat SOP and use standardized templates. This responds to A1.4, which is already rated 4, while addressing the need to document the existing practice more systematically.\n\n### **7.3 Process 3: Institutional Policy and Ordinance Development**\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure that local DRRM policies are legally sound, risk-informed, coordinated and implementable.\n\nThe process shall be used for the proposed CDRRMO enabling ordinance, updated CDRRMC Executive Order, EOC establishment issuance, ICS institutionalization and amendments to existing DRRM ordinances.\n\n**Workflow GOV-03: Policy Development and Approval**\n\n1.  **Identify the policy gap.** CDRRMO documents the legal, institutional or operational deficiency.\n\n2.  **Develop the technical proposal**. Prepare the proposed policy, institutional implications and implementation requirements.\n\n3.  **Conduct consultation.** Engage affected City offices, CDRRMC members, CSOs and relevant partners.\n\n4.  **Legal review.** The City Legal Office reviews legality, authority, consistency and enforceability.\n\n5.  **Resource review**. HRMO and Budget Office assess staffing, funding and administrative implications.\n\n6.  **Approva**l. Submit the proposal to the Mayor or Sangguniang Panlungsod, depending on the nature of the instrument.\n\n7.  **Publication and dissemination**. Circulate the approved policy to responsible offices and affected stakeholders.\n\n8.  **Implementation and evaluation**. Monitor compliance, assess effectiveness and recommend revisions.\n\n**Decision rule**: The appropriate approving authority shall be determined by the legal nature of the proposed instrument. An ordinance shall not be substituted by an Executive Order where legislative action is required.\n\n### **7.4 Process 4: State of Calamity Declaration**\n\nThe City shall maintain a documented protocol consistent with Section 16 of RA 10121 and its IRR.\n\nA State of Calamity declaration is based on the results of damage assessment and needs analysis. It shall be distinguished from the anticipatory declaration mechanism under RA 12287.\n\n**Workflow GOV-04: State of Calamity**\n\n1.  **Incident assessment**  \n    > BDRRMCs, response agencies and the CDRRMO report casualties, affected populations, infrastructure damage, livelihood disruption and service interruptions.\n\n2.  **RDANA consolidation**  \n    > CDRRMO coordinates the validation and consolidation of damage and needs information.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation  \n    > **CDRRMO prepares the assessment and submits it to the CDRRMC.\n\n4.  **CDRRMC deliberation**  \n    > The Council evaluates the evidence and adopts a recommendation where the legal criteria are met.\n\n5.  **Legislative declaration  \n    > **The Sangguniang Panlungsod considers and issues the declaration or lifting through the appropriate resolution.\n\n6.  **Implementation  \n    > **The City Government implements applicable remedial measures, resource arrangements, assistance and reporting requirements.\n\n7.  **Monitoring and lifting  \n    > **The CDRRMO monitors continuing impacts and prepares the technical basis for recommending lifting when appropriate.\n\n**Required Outputs**\n\nRDANA, CDRRMC recommendation, Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution, implementation records and lifting documentation.\n\nThe City shall maintain the following supporting records:\n\n- Technical assessment and recommendation;\n\n- RDANA and other supporting reports;\n\n- CDRRMC resolution;\n\n- Sangguniang Panlungsod resolution;\n\n- Post-declaration action register;\n\n- Resource utilization and accountability records; and\n\n- Lifting recommendation and corresponding resolution.\n\nThe protocol shall also address coordination where national or regional declarations, infrastructure closures or emergency restrictions affect Ormoc City without automatically constituting a local State of Calamity declaration.\n\n### **7.5 Process 5: State of Imminent Disaster Declaration and Anticipatory Action**\n\n**New statutory Governance Process**\n\nRA 12287 establishes a mechanism for declaring a State of Imminent Disaster before a disaster occurs, based on the applicable forecast, pre-disaster risk assessment and legal criteria.\n\nThe IRR released on 29 September 2026 provides the implementing framework for this mechanism.\n\nThe assessment identifies A1.8 as a mandatory requirement with a score of 1. The City has no localized procedure, request template or Mayor''s Executive Order template documented in the evidence reviewed.\n\nThe following workflow shall therefore be institutionalized.\n\n**Workflow GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster**\n\n1.  **Hazard forecast and trigger monitoring  \n    > **CDRRMO Operations and Warning monitors official forecasts, advisories and hazard-specific triggers from PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent agencies.\n\n2.  **Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment  \n    > **CDRRMO coordinates the assessment of exposure, vulnerability, projected severity, likely impacts, affected sectors, lead time and available response capacity.\n\n3.  **Technical recommendation**  \n    > CDRRMO prepares the PDRA findings and proposed anticipatory measures for CDRRMC consideration.\n\n4.  **Regional coordination and recommendation  \n    > **The City coordinates with RDRRMC VIII through OCD Region VIII and submits the prescribed request and supporting documentation.\n\n5.  **Regional decision and response**  \n    > RDRRMC VIII undertakes the action required under the Act and IRR. The request, acknowledgment and prescribed response timeline are documented.\n\n6.  **Local declaration**  \n    > Upon the applicable recommendation, the City Mayor may declare a State of Imminent Disaster through an Executive Order covering the jurisdiction forecast to be affected.\n\n7.  **Activation of anticipatory measures**  \n    > Authorized offices implement approved early actions, including pre-positioning, evacuation, protection of critical infrastructure and targeted social assistance.\n\n8.  **Funding and accountability  \n    > **Budget, Accounting, Procurement and implementing offices document commitments, disbursements, utilization and outputs in accordance with the IRR and applicable DBM-COA rules.\n\n9.  **Lifting and post-declaration reporting  \n    > **The City observes the applicable lifting procedures and submits the required post-lifting report to OCD within the prescribed 45-day period.\n\nThe process shall observe the following safeguards:\n\n1.  A forecast alone shall not automatically trigger a declaration. The applicable legal criteria and PDRA requirements must be met.\n\n2.  The CDRRMO shall provide technical recommendations, not independently exercise the declaration authority.\n\n3.  The local declaration shall follow the recommendation and coordination mechanism prescribed by RA 12287 and its IRR.\n\n4.  Anticipatory actions shall be proportionate to the projected impacts, hazard lead time, available resources and applicable legal authority.\n\n5.  Measures shall observe inclusion, protection, do-no-harm and no-regrets principles.\n\n6.  The use of funds shall follow the statutory funding limits, tranche arrangements and applicable financial regulations.\n\n7.  Decisions, approvals, actions and expenditures shall be documented for audit and evaluation.\n\n**Funding control**: As reflected in the assessment workbook, the IRR provides for an anticipatory-action allocation of up to 10% of the 70% LDRRMF component and a 30%/70% tranche arrangement associated with the prescribed three-day lead-time rule. The precise timing, eligibility, disbursement and documentation requirements must be implemented using the certified IRR and applicable DBM-COA guidance. The MOO shall not treat this allocation as an additional fund outside the statutory LDRRMF framework.\n\n**Independent Component City clarification:** Ormoc''s status as an Independent Component City must be considered when establishing its vertical coordination arrangements. The City shall obtain written confirmation from OCD Region VIII on the application of the IRR''s regional and provincial coordination provisions, particularly whether any provincial declaration has legal applicability to Ormoc. The City''s ICC status shall not be used as a reason to bypass the RDRRMC recommendation mechanism expressly established by RA 12287.\n\n### **7.6 Process 6: Institutional Escalation and Inter-agency Coordination**\n\nInstitutional escalation shall occur when:\n\n- The scale of an incident exceeds the capacity of a barangay;\n\n- Multiple barangays require coordinated intervention;\n\n- City resources are insufficient;\n\n- Specialized national or regional assistance is required;\n\n- Critical infrastructure or essential services are disrupted; or\n\n- A statutory declaration or executive decision is necessary.\n\nRA 10121, Section 15, provides the general coordination framework: barangay-level leadership where a barangay is affected, city/municipal leadership where two or more barangays are affected, and progressively higher coordination levels when impacts exceed the relevant territorial scale. Higher councils and institutions support the LGU''s primary response responsibility.\n\nThe operational workflow shall be:\n\n1.  Incident or risk information is reported to the appropriate local authority.\n\n2.  The affected BDRRMC undertakes initial action and reports to the City.\n\n3.  CDRRMO validates the information and assesses the level of support required.\n\n4.  The CDRRMC and EOC coordinate City-wide institutional action, as appropriate.\n\n5.  The City requests external assistance through the prescribed channels when required.\n\n6.  The receiving institution acknowledges and assigns the request.\n\n7.  The CDRRMO monitors the response and maintains the institutional record.\n\n8.  Unresolved requirements are escalated through the appropriate higher-level coordination mechanism.\n\n### **7.7 Process 7: Governance Review and Corrective Action**\n\nAll governance processes shall be subject to periodic review.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall:\n\n1.  Consolidate compliance and performance information;\n\n2.  Compare actual results against approved targets;\n\n3.  Identify gaps, recurring problems and systemic causes;\n\n4.  Assign corrective actions to accountable offices;\n\n5.  Establish completion dates and required resources;\n\n6.  Verify implementation;\n\n7.  Report unresolved issues to the CDRRMC; and\n\n8.  Update policies and procedures when necessary.\n\nThis process shall link the Institutional Assessment Tool, Action Plan, CDRRMC action register, annual LDRRMP monitoring and the MOO revision mechanism.\n\n## **8. Responsibility Assignment and RACI Framework**\n\n### **8.1 Application**\n\nThe RACI framework shall be used to clarify institutional responsibility for governance processes and prevent overlapping mandates, unassigned tasks and ambiguous decision authority.\n\nThe following definitions apply:\n\n| **Code** | **Responsibility** | **Application**                                                               |\n|----------|--------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| R        | Responsible        | Performs the work and produces the required output.                           |\n| A        | Accountable        | Owns the result and approves the output or decision. Only one A per activity. |\n| **C**    | Consulted          | Provides technical, legal or operational advice before the decision.          |\n| **I**    | Informed           | Receives relevant information on the decision or outcome.                     |\n\nRACI assignments shall be interpreted subject to statutory powers, formal delegations and approved organizational arrangements. A RACI designation does not create authority that does not otherwise exist under law.\n\n### **8.2 Institutional Roles Used in the RACI Matrix**\n\n| **Role**                             | **Abbreviation** | **Principal responsibility**                                                        |\n|--------------------------------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Local Chief Executive                | Mayor            | Executive authority and statutory decisions.                                        |\n| Sangguniang Panlungsod               | SP               | Legislative approval and local declaration authority.                               |\n| CDRRMC                               | Council          | Collective policy direction, deliberation and recommendation.                       |\n| CDRRMO Head                          | CDRRMO           | Permanent office leadership and technical accountability.                           |\n| CDRRMO Secretariat                   | Secretariat      | Council administration, documentation and action tracking.                          |\n| City Legal Office                    | CLO              | Legal review and compliance advice.                                                 |\n| City Planning and Development Office | CPDO             | Integration of DRR/CCA into development planning.                                   |\n| HRMO                                 | HRMO             | Staffing, qualification and personnel administration.                               |\n| City Budget / Accounting / BAC       | Finance          | Budgeting, accounting, procurement and financial control.                           |\n| City Information Office              | CIO              | Public information and risk communication support.                                  |\n| BDRRMCs                              | Barangays        | Community-level governance, reporting and local action.                             |\n| City departments and agencies        | Departments      | Sectoral implementation and technical support.                                      |\n| OCD / RDRRMC VIII                    | Regional         | Regional coordination, technical assistance and statutory recommendation functions. |\n| EOC Manager / IMT                    | EOC/IMT          | Emergency coordination and incident-management interface.                           |\n| CSOs / Private Sector                | Partners         | Community participation, technical support and resource commitments.                |\n\n### \n\n### **8.3 Master RACI Matrix – institutional Governance**\n\nThe following matrix establishes the proposed responsibility assignments for Chapter 1.\n\n| **Governance activity**                                   | **Mayor** | **SP** | **CDRRMC** | **CDRRMO** | **Secretariat** | **CLO** | **HRMO / Finance** | **OCD VIII** |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------|------------|------------|-----------------|---------|--------------------|--------------|\n| Establish CDRRMO through ordinance                        | I         | A      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC composition EO                             | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Maintain CDRRMC membership register                       | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare CDRRMC agenda and meetings                        | I         | I      | A          | **C**      | R               | I       | I                  | I            |\n| Document Council resolutions                              | I         | I      | A          | **C**      | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Monitor Council decisions                                 | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | I       | **C**              | I            |\n| Develop local DRRM policy                                 | A         | **C**  | **C**      | R          | **C**           | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Review proposed DRRM ordinances                           | **C**     | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | A       | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Prepare State of Calamity technical assessment            | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | R               | **C**   | **C**              | I            |\n| Recommend State of Calamity                               | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Issue local State of Calamity declaration                 | I         | A      | R          | C          | I               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Prepare State of Imminent Disaster PDRA                   | I         | I      | **C**      | A          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Submit request to RDRRMC VIII                             | I         | I      | A          | R          | **C**           | **C**   | I                  | **C**        |\n| Recommend regional State of Imminent Disaster declaration | I         | I      | **C**      | **C**      | I               | I       | I                  | A/R\\*        |\n| Issue local State of Imminent Disaster EO                 | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n| Coordinate anticipatory action                            | A         | I      | **C**      | R          | I               | **C**   | R                  | **C**        |\n| Prepare 45-day post-lifting report                        | I         | I      | **C**      | A/R        | **C**           | **C**   | R                  | I            |\n| Maintain legal and policy register                        | I         | I      | I          | A          | R               | **C**   | I                  | I            |\n| Conduct annual governance review                          | I         | I      | A          | R          | R               | **C**   | **C**              | **C**        |\n\n\\*The RACI assignment for OCD/RDRRMC VIII reflects the regional recommendation function described in RA 12287. The exact responsible regional body and administrative sequence shall be finalized against the certified IRR and applicable regional procedures. The RACI matrix does not replace the statutory decision authority of the NDRRMC, regional councils, Mayor or Sanggunian.\n\n### **8.4 RACI Matrix – institutional interfaces**\n\nPart C of the assessment shall be operationalized through the following responsibility arrangements.\n\n| **Interface activity**                  | **Accountable owner**                        | **Responsible actors**             | **CDRRMO role**                                 |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRA and CLUP integration               | CPDO                                         | CPDO technical team                | C / technical input                             |\n| DRR/CCA project screening               | CPDO                                         | CPDO, City Budget Office           | C / risk validation                             |\n| Critical facility structural assessment | City Engineer / Building Official            | Engineering and inspection teams   | C / risk prioritization                         |\n| Utility continuity agreements           | Relevant utility / designated City authority | Utility providers and City offices | R for coordination, subject to approved mandate |\n| Watershed and ecosystem monitoring      | CENRO                                        | CENRO, DENR and partners           | C / DRRM integration                            |\n| Health anticipatory action              | City Health Officer                          | CHO, hospitals and health partners | C / coordination                                |\n| Education anticipatory action           | DepEd Schools Division                       | School DRRM coordinators           | C / coordination                                |\n| Agriculture and fisheries early action  | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office         | CAO, CVO and relevant agencies     | C / coordination                                |\n| Price monitoring                        | DTI / City Market Office                     | Market monitoring personnel        | C / EOC information integration                 |\n| Business continuity outreach            | CDRRMO Head                                  | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce     | A / R for DRRM outreach                         |\n\nThis matrix ensures that the CDRRMO coordinates the overall resilience system without appropriating the mandates of other City offices.\n\n### **8.5 RACI Compliance Rules**\n\nThe following rules shall govern all RACI matrices in the MOO:\n\n1.  Each activity shall have one clearly designated Accountable role.\n\n2.  Multiple Responsible roles may be assigned where work is genuinely shared.\n\n3.  Consulted roles shall be limited to those whose technical or institutional input is necessary.\n\n4.  Informed roles shall receive the relevant outputs without becoming unnecessary approval points.\n\n5.  RACI assignments shall be reviewed whenever the organizational structure, legal mandate or delegation changes.\n\n6.  Where a statutory body exercises collective authority, the Council or Sanggunian shall be identified as the accountable decision-making body, with the designated Chairperson responsible for convening and presiding over proceedings.\n\n7.  Each RACI matrix shall identify the approving authority for the final output.\n\nThe detailed job action sheets and position-level RACI matrices shall be developed under the organizational and EOC modules.\n\n## **9. Total Quality Management and Institutional Performance**\n\n### **9.1 Quality Management Approach**\n\nThe CDRRMO shall apply Total Quality Management principles to the design, implementation, monitoring and improvement of its governance processes.\n\nTQM shall be treated as an institutional management approach rather than a separate administrative activity. Its purpose is to ensure that DRRM services are reliable, timely, accountable, evidence-based and responsive to the needs of affected communities.\n\nThe following principles shall guide implementation:\n\n| **TQM principle**              | **Application to CDRRMO–EOC operations**                                                                                       |\n|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Customer and stakeholder focus | Decisions and services shall respond to the needs of communities, affected populations, responders and institutional partners. |\n| Leadership and accountability  | Each process shall have a designated owner and authorized decision-maker.                                                      |\n| Process orientation            | Activities shall be standardized through workflows, SOPs, checklists and defined outputs.                                      |\n| Systems approach               | Institutional processes shall be managed as interconnected functions rather than isolated activities.                          |\n| Evidence-based decisions       | Decisions shall be supported by verified risk information, assessment results, operational reports and documented evidence.    |\n| People engagement              | Personnel, barangays, CSOs and partners shall participate in process design, implementation and improvement.                   |\n| Continuous improvement         | Findings from exercises, incidents, audits and assessments shall result in corrective and preventive action.                   |\n| Relationship management        | Partnerships and institutional interfaces shall be actively maintained and evaluated.                                          |\n\n### **9.2 Plan–Do–Check–Act (PDCA) cycle**\n\nThe City shall apply the PDCA cycle as the primary mechanism for institutional quality improvement.\n\nInstitutional quality management cycle\n\n# **PLAN**\n\nEstablish standards\n\nIdentify risks, set targets, assign owners, approve procedures and allocate resources.\n\n# **DO**\n\nImplement processes\n\nExecute SOPs, coordinate actors, document outputs and deliver services.\n\n# **CHECK**\n\nMeasure performance\n\nMonitor indicators, verify compliance, review records and evaluate results.\n\n# **ACT**\n\nCorrect and improve\n\nAddress root causes, revise procedures, assign corrective actions and retest improvements.\n\nThe PDCA cycle shall operate continuously across all CDRRMO and EOC functional modules.\n\n### **9.3 Application of PDCA to institutional governance**\n\n| **PDCA stage** | **Key activities**                                                                                    | **Responsible institutional actors**              | **Required evidence**                                     |\n|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n| Plan           | Annual governance calendar, policy review, risk-informed priorities, budget and staffing requirements | CDRRMO Head, CDRRMC, City Planning, Budget Office | Approved plans, meeting calendar, LDRRMFIP                |\n| Do             | Meetings, policy implementation, coordination, declaration procedures and institutional operations    | CDRRMO, Secretariat, City offices and partners    | Minutes, resolutions, SOP records, reports                |\n| Check          | Compliance assessment, performance monitoring, document verification and review of decisions          | CDRRMO, Internal Audit, CDRRMC                    | Dashboard, audit reports, compliance register             |\n| Act            | Corrective action, institutional restructuring, policy amendment and procedure revision               | Mayor, CDRRMC, SP and responsible offices         | Corrective action register, revised policies, updated MOO |\n\n### **9.4 Process Performance Indicators**\n\nThe following indicators are proposed for Chapter 1. They shall be integrated into the broader MOO Monitoring and Evaluation Framework.\n\n| **Performance indicator**                                                          | **Proposed target**                        | **Frequency**        | **Process owner**  |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------|\n| CDRRMC regular meetings conducted                                                  | At least 4 annually                        | Quarterly            | CDRRMC Secretariat |\n| CDRRMC membership register updated                                                 | 100% of prescribed seats documented        | Annual               | CDRRMO             |\n| CDRRMC resolutions with assigned owners and deadlines                              | 100%                                       | Every meeting        | Secretariat        |\n| CDRRMC action items with documented status                                         | 100% tracked                               | Quarterly            | CDRRMO             |\n| Critical DRRM policies subjected to legal review                                   | 100% before approval                       | Per policy           | City Legal Office  |\n| Critical governance procedures with approved SOPs                                  | 100% of identified mandatory procedures    | Annual review        | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Calamity recommendations with supporting RDANA                            | 100%                                       | Per occurrence       | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Imminent Disaster requests with complete PDRA documentation               | 100%                                       | Per applicable event | CDRRMO             |\n| State of Imminent Disaster post-lifting reports submitted within prescribed period | 100%                                       | Per declaration      | CDRRMO             |\n| CDRRMC decisions with documented follow-through                                    | 100% recorded                              | Quarterly            | CDRRMO Secretariat |\n| High-priority institutional corrective actions completed by approved deadlines     | At least 90%, with overdue items escalated | Quarterly            | CDRRMO Head        |\n\nThese are proposed management targets, not existing performance claims. They shall be calibrated during the validation workshop and approved through the City''s institutional performance arrangements.\n\n### **9.5 Corrective and Preventive Action**\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a Corrective and Preventive Action Register (CAPA) linked to:\n\n- Institutional Assessment Tool findings;\n\n- CDRRMC resolutions;\n\n- Internal and external audit findings;\n\n- After-action reviews;\n\n- Exercise evaluation reports;\n\n- Incident and response reports;\n\n- Stakeholder feedback; and\n\n- Applicable changes in laws and policies.\n\nEach corrective action shall identify:\n\n| **CAPA field**       | **Required information**                                                                   |\n|----------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Reference            | Source of the finding or non-conformity                                                    |\n| Problem statement    | Specific deficiency or process failure                                                     |\n| Root cause           | Underlying organizational, procedural, technical or resource-related cause                 |\n| Risk consequence     | Potential effect on life safety, service delivery, compliance or institutional performance |\n| Corrective action    | Specific intervention to address the deficiency                                            |\n| Accountable owner    | Single office or official responsible for completion                                       |\n| Responsible actors   | Personnel or offices undertaking the work                                                  |\n| Target date          | Approved completion deadline                                                               |\n| Required resources   | Budget, personnel, equipment or technical assistance                                       |\n| Verification         | Evidence that the action was implemented                                                   |\n| Effectiveness review | Evidence that the original problem has been resolved and has not recurred                  |\n\nA finding shall not be closed merely because an action has been initiated. Closure requires evidence of completion and verification of effectiveness.\n\n## **10. Alignment with the MCR2030 Ten Essentials**\n\nThis chapter primarily contributes to the Governance and Financial Capacity cluster of the MCR2030 Ten Essentials while establishing the institutional foundation for the other two clusters.\n\n| **MCR2030 Essential**                                              | **Chapter 1 contribution** | **Institutional mechanism**                                                             |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Organize for Disaster Resilience                               | Primary                    | CDRRMC, CDRRMO, EOC mandates, authorities and coordination architecture.                |\n| 2\\. Identify, Understand and Use Current and Future Risk Scenarios | Supporting                 | Institutional responsibility for risk intelligence and evidence-based policy decisions. |\n| 3\\. Strengthen Financial Capability for Resilience                 | Supporting                 | Governance of DRRM investment, funding, accountability and resource mobilization.       |\n| 4\\. Pursue Resilient Urban Development and Design                  | Supporting                 | Institutional interface with CPDO, Zoning Administrator and City Engineer.              |\n| 5\\. Safeguard Natural Buffers to Enhance Protective Functions      | Supporting                 | Institutional interface with CENRO, DENR and watershed partners.                        |\n| 6\\. Strengthen Institutional Capacity for Resilience               | Primary                    | Institutional roles, accountability, staffing, competence and continuity.               |\n| 7\\. Understand and Strengthen Societal Capacity for Resilience     | Primary                    | CDRRMC representation, barangay participation, CSO and private-sector partnerships.     |\n| 8\\. Increase Infrastructure Resilience                             | Supporting                 | Cross-office coordination on critical facilities and lifeline continuity.               |\n| 9\\. Ensure Effective Disaster Response                             | Primary                    | EOC mandate, coordination arrangements and institutional decision pathways.             |\n| 10\\. Expedite Recovery and Build Back Better                       | Supporting                 | Governance continuity and accountability for recovery and reconstruction.               |\n\nThe City shall use this alignment to ensure that institutional improvements contribute to the wider urban resilience agenda, not merely to the internal functionality of the CDRRMO and EOC.\n\n## **11. Institutional Improvement Priorities Arising from the Assessment**\n\nThe following actions are directly linked to the assessment findings and shall be included in the Institutional Improvement Action Plan.\n\n| **Priority** | **Assessment reference** | **Required action**                                                                                                                    | **Accountable owner**                                      | **Supporting actors**                        | **Target indicated** |\n|--------------|--------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| High         | A1.1                     | Enact an ordinance establishing the CDRRMO as a regular department, with prescribed divisions and functions.                           | Mayor / SP, according to legislative process               | CDRRMO, CLO, HRMO                            | 2027                 |\n| Medium       | A1.2                     | Update CDRRMC composition to meet statutory membership requirements.                                                                   | Mayor                                                      | CDRRMO, CLO, accredited CSOs, private sector | 2027                 |\n| Medium       | A1.6                     | Develop a documented State of Calamity procedure and supporting templates.                                                             | CDRRMO Head                                                | CDRRMC, CLO, RDANA team                      | January 2027         |\n| High         | A1.8                     | Establish the State of Imminent Disaster procedure, including regional coordination, request and EO templates, lifting and reporting.  | Mayor for declaration; CDRRMO Head for process development | CDRRMC, OCD VIII, CLO, Budget Office         | January 2027         |\n| High         | B1.1                     | Issue an Executive Order establishing the City EOC and defining its relationship with CDRRMC and ICS.                                  | Mayor                                                      | CDRRMO, CLO, CDRRMC                          | January 2027         |\n| High         | A1.7 / A4.6              | Use existing City Development Council and Local Finance Committee representation to secure anticipatory-action financing arrangements. | Mayor / appropriate finance approving authorities          | CDRRMO, Budget Office, Accounting, BAC       | FY2028 budget        |\n| Medium       | A1.3 / A1.4              | Institutionalize CDRRMC Secretariat procedures and action tracking while maintaining quarterly meetings.                               | CDRRMO Head                                                | Secretariat, CDRRMC members                  | January 2027         |\n| Medium       | A1.5                     | Review and amend evacuation and other DRRM-related local ordinances, including hazard-based provisions.                                | Mayor / SP, according to instrument                        | CDRRMO, CLO, CPDO, barangays                 | 2027                 |\n\nThe action plan should retain the detailed status, funding, target dates and evidence requirements in the workbook. This chapter establishes the governance requirements and ownership; it does not replace the Action Plan.\n\n### **11.1 Priority Sequencing**\n\nThe improvement actions shall be implemented in the following sequence:\n\nPhase 1 – Legal and institutional foundation\n\n- CDRRMO enabling ordinance;\n\n- Updated CDRRMC Executive Order;\n\n- EOC establishment issuance;\n\n- State of Imminent Disaster SOP;\n\n- State of Calamity SOP.\n\nPhase 2 – Operational institutionalization\n\n- Secretariat SOP and action register;\n\n- Formal inter-agency coordination and delegation arrangements;\n\n- Approved CDRRMO and EOC role descriptions;\n\n- Institutional policy and records register.\n\nPhase 3 – Performance and continuous improvement\n\n- CDRRMC governance performance monitoring;\n\n- CAPA implementation;\n\n- Periodic legal and policy review;\n\n- Validation and testing of institutional procedures.\n\n## **12. Required SOPs, Forms and Institutional Records**\n\nThe following instruments shall be maintained under this chapter.\n\n| **Code** | **SOP / Instrument**                               | **Required output**                                      |\n|----------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|\n| GOV-01   | CDRRMC governance and meeting procedure            | Annual calendar, notices, agendas and minutes            |\n| GOV-02   | CDRRMC Secretariat management                      | Secretariat register and document-control system         |\n| GOV-03   | Institutional policy and ordinance development     | Policy development and legal review checklist            |\n| GOV-04   | State of Calamity declaration and lifting          | RDANA-based recommendation and declaration package       |\n| GOV-05   | State of Imminent Disaster declaration and lifting | PDRA, regional request, Mayor''s EO and reporting package |\n| GOV-06   | Inter-agency and vertical coordination             | Coordination protocol and contact directory              |\n| GOV-07   | RACI and delegation management                     | Responsibility and authority register                    |\n| GOV-08   | Institutional records management                   | Controlled document and records register                 |\n| GOV-09   | Governance performance monitoring                  | KPI dashboard and action register                        |\n| GOV-10   | Corrective and preventive action management        | CAPA register and verification reports                   |\n\nThe following templates shall be included in the MOO annexes:\n\n- GOV-F01 – CDRRMC Membership and Designation Register;\n\n- GOV-F02 – CDRRMC Meeting Notice and Agenda;\n\n- GOV-F03 – CDRRMC Attendance and Minutes;\n\n- GOV-F04 – CDRRMC Resolution Template;\n\n- GOV-F05 – Resolution and Action Monitoring Register;\n\n- GOV-F06 – Institutional Policy Review Checklist;\n\n- GOV-F07 – State of Calamity Technical Assessment;\n\n- GOV-F08 – State of Calamity Recommendation and Resolution;\n\n- GOV-F09 – State of Imminent Disaster PDRA Summary;\n\n- GOV-F10 – Request to RDRRMC VIII;\n\n- GOV-F11 – Mayor''s State of Imminent Disaster Executive Order Template;\n\n- GOV-F12 – State of Imminent Disaster Lifting Documentation;\n\n- GOV-F13 – Post-Lifting 45-Day Report;\n\n- GOV-F14 – Institutional RACI Register;\n\n- GOV-F15 – Corrective and Preventive Action Register;\n\n- GOV-F16 – Legal and Policy Register.\n\nAll templates shall have document identifiers, version numbers, approval status, responsible custodian and review dates.\n\n## **13. Governance Controls, Documentation and Records Management**\n\nThe CDRRMO shall establish a controlled documentation system to ensure that institutional decisions and procedures remain accessible, authentic, traceable and current.\n\n### **13.1 Document Control**\n\nEach controlled document shall contain:\n\n- Document title and code;\n\n- Version and revision number;\n\n- Date of preparation;\n\n- Prepared-by and reviewed-by designations;\n\n- Approving authority;\n\n- Effective date;\n\n- Revision history; and\n\n- Distribution and access classification.\n\nOnly approved versions shall be used as official operational references.\n\n### **13.2 Records Management**\n\nThe CDRRMO Secretariat shall maintain the official governance records in coordination with the City Records Officer.\n\nRecords shall be classified into:\n\n1.  Legal and policy records;\n\n2.  CDRRMC governance records;\n\n3.  Institutional organizational records;\n\n4.  Declaration and executive decision records;\n\n5.  Financial and resource accountability records;\n\n6.  Coordination and partnership records;\n\n7.  Performance and audit records; and\n\n8.  Corrective action and improvement records.\n\nRetention and disposition shall follow applicable government records management and archival requirements.\n\n### **13.3 Data Protection and Information Sharing**\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate with the City''s Data Protection Officer and information technology office to establish appropriate safeguards for DRRM information.\n\nThe system shall distinguish:\n\n- Publicly available risk and preparedness information;\n\n- Internal institutional information;\n\n- Restricted operational information;\n\n- Personal information of affected populations;\n\n- Sensitive personal information; and\n\n- Confidential personnel and security records.\n\nThe EOC shall use aggregated and appropriately anonymized information for operational dashboards whenever personal identifiers are not required.\n\nData sharing with CSWDO, CPDO, national agencies, barangays and partners shall be governed by appropriate data-sharing agreements and privacy safeguards under RA 10173.\n\n## **14. Implementation, Review and Institutional Assurance**\n\n### **14.1 Institutional Ownership**\n\nThe CDRRMO Head shall be the overall process owner for the implementation of this chapter, under the administrative authority of the Local Chief Executive and the policy oversight of the CDRRMC.\n\nThe City Legal Office, HRMO, Budget Office, Accounting Office, Secretariat and other concerned institutions shall discharge their assigned responsibilities.\n\n### **14.2 Review Schedule**\n\n| **Review activity**              | **Frequency**                                      | **Responsible owner**      |\n|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| CDRRMC membership review         | Annually and after material organizational changes | CDRRMO                     |\n| Governance SOP review            | Annually and after significant policy changes      | CDRRMO Head                |\n| Legal and policy register review | At least annually                                  | CDRRMO / City Legal Office |\n| RACI and delegation review       | Annually and after structural changes              | CDRRMO / HRMO              |\n| CDRRMC performance review        | Quarterly, with annual consolidation               | CDRRMC Secretariat         |\n| Institutional assessment         | Annually, or after major institutional changes     | CDRRMO                     |\n| CAPA review                      | Quarterly                                          | CDRRMO Head                |\n| MOO revision                     | Annually or when necessary                         | CDRRMO Head                |\n\n### **14.3 Institutional Assurance**\n\nThe implementation of this chapter shall be evaluated through three complementary tests:\n\nCompliance test: Are statutory and regulatory requirements met?\n\nEffectiveness test: Do institutional arrangements produce timely, coordinated and reliable decisions and outputs?\n\nSustainability test: Are the arrangements institutionalized, documented, resourced, tested and capable of surviving leadership and personnel changes?\n\nA governance process shall be considered effective only when it satisfies all three dimensions appropriate to its function.\n\n# **15. Technical Validation Notes and Outstanding Decisions**\n\nThe following matters should be resolved before Chapter 1 is submitted for formal approval.\n\n| **Validation issue**       | **Required action**                                                                                                                                        | **Responsible institution**  |\n|----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO legal status        | Confirm existing EO and prepare enabling ordinance.                                                                                                        | City Legal Office / SP       |\n| CDRRMC composition         | Verify all statutory seats and CSO accreditation.                                                                                                          | CDRRMO / Mayor''s Office      |\n| State of Calamity          | Validate local declaration and lifting templates against RA 10121 Section 16.                                                                              | City Legal Office            |\n| State of Imminent Disaster | Confirm certified IRR provisions, RDRRMC VIII request route, response timelines and local EO procedure.                                                    | OCD VIII / City Legal Office |\n| ICC coordination           | Confirm applicability of provincial coordination provisions to Ormoc.                                                                                      | OCD VIII                     |\n| EOC establishment          | Verify NDRRMC MC No. 131, s. 2018 and the exact issuance title and provisions.                                                                             | OCD VIII                     |\n| EOC–ICS interface          | Confirm the relationship between EOC, IMT, Incident Commander and response clusters.                                                                       | CDRRMO / OCD VIII            |\n| Organizational structure   | Reconcile JMC 2014-1, approved plantilla and proposed CDRRMO department structure.                                                                         | HRMO / CDRRMO                |\n| Institutional maturity     | Validate provisional scores and resolve contradictory evidence.                                                                                            | Assessment team / CDRRMO     |\n| Version control            | The uploaded filename identifies v1.3, while the Guide sheet still identifies the instrument as v1.1. Reconcile the workbook version and revision history. | Assessment team              |\n\nTwo important corrections incorporated into this revised chapter:\n\n1.  A State of Calamity is declared by the local sanggunian upon CDRRMC recommendation, not simply through a Mayor''s Executive Order.\n\n2.  A State of Imminent Disaster is a separate anticipatory mechanism under RA 12287. The Local Chief Executive may declare it through an Executive Order upon the prescribed Regional DRRM Council recommendation.\n\nThese distinctions are essential to legal compliance.\n\n## **16. Relationship with the Succeeding Chapters**\n\nTo avoid duplication and maintain a coherent Manual, the following division of responsibilities is recommended:\n\n| **Chapter** | **Subject**                                                             | **Principal assessment references** |\n|-------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| 1           | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability         | A1, B1.1, C1–C5 interfaces          |\n| 2           | CDRRMO Organizational Structure, Staffing and Human Resource Management | A2, B4                              |\n| 3           | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness              | A3, A5, relevant Part C indicators  |\n| 4           | Early Warning, Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response   | A6, A7, A8, B1.2–B1.5               |\n| 5           | DRRM Financing, Procurement and Resource Accountability                 | A4                                  |\n| 6           | EOC Operations, Information Management, Logistics and Coordination      | B2–B7, relevant A indicators        |\n| 7           | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement | A5.4–A5.9, A10                      |\n| 8           | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                          | A9                                  |\n| 9           | Partnerships, Local Ownership and Institutional Sustainability          | A10, relevant Part C indicators     |\n\nThis structure should be reconciled with the ten functional areas in Annex A and the eight contractual functional modules. The chapter numbering is a proposed detailed document architecture, not a change to the contractual scope.\n\n## **16. Concluding Notes**\n\nThis chapter establishes the legal, institutional and governance foundation for the integrated operation of Ormoc City''s CDRRMC, CDRRMO and EOC.\n\nIt recognizes that urban resilience is not produced by a single office or emergency facility. It emerges from the effective interaction of executive leadership, institutional capacity, policy coordination, risk information, financial governance, community participation and operational systems.\n\nThe institutional assessment demonstrates that Ormoc has established important DRRM governance practices, particularly regular CDRRMC meetings, Secretariat functions, DRRM representation in development and financial bodies, and existing planning and partnership mechanisms. However, gaps in legal institutionalization, EOC authority, anticipatory governance and cross-office coordination continue to constrain the effectiveness of the wider system.\n\nThe provisions of this chapter respond to these issues by establishing:\n\n- Clear institutional mandates and decision rights;\n\n- Defined process ownership and responsibility assignments;\n\n- Standardized governance workflows;\n\n- Legally differentiated declaration procedures;\n\n- Formalized institutional and inter-agency interfaces;\n\n- RACI-based accountability;\n\n- TQM and PDCA-based quality assurance; and\n\n- Evidence-based monitoring and continuous improvement.\n\nThe ultimate objective is to move Ormoc City''s DRRM governance from arrangements that are partly dependent on individual initiative toward a legally grounded, process-driven, integrated and continuously improving institutional system.\n\nThis foundation shall support the implementation of the succeeding modules, particularly organizational management, preparedness, EOC operations, information management, resource mobilization, recovery and performance improvement.\n\n# **References**\n\nThe following references provide the legal and technical foundation for this chapter. The final bibliography should be consolidated with the complete MOO reference register.\n\n- Department of Budget and Management, Department of the Interior and Local Government, Civil Service Commission, & National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2014). Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1: Implementing guidelines for the establishment of Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Offices or Barangay Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Committees in Local Government Units. [<u>Official DBM issuance</u>](https://www.dbm.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/Issuances/2014/Joint%20Memorandum%20Circular/JMC2014-1%20_LDRRMOs.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2012). Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012: Implementing guidelines on the use of the Incident Command System.\n\n- National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2026). Implementing Rules and Regulations of Republic Act No. 12287. Signed September 14, 2026, as identified in the assessment workbook.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (1991). Republic Act No. 7160: Local Government Code of 1991.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2009). Republic Act No. 9729: Climate Change Act of 2009.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2010). Republic Act No. 10121: Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010. [<u>Full text of RA 10121</u>](https://lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2010/ra_10121_2010.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2012). Republic Act No. 10173: Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2018). Republic Act No. 11036: Mental Health Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12076: Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2024). Republic Act No. 12009: New Government Procurement Act.\n\n- Republic of the Philippines. (2025). Republic Act No. 12287: Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act. [<u>Official legislative reference</u>](https://ldr.senate.gov.ph/legislative-issuance/republic-act-no-12287?utm_source=chatgpt.com).\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities: Detailed level assessment.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2018). Making cities resilient 2030: Ten Essentials for Making Cities Resilient.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2015). ISO 9001:2015: Quality management systems—Requirements.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2018). ISO 31000:2018: Risk management—Guidelines.\n\n- International Organization for Standardization. (2019). ISO 22301:2019: Security and resilience—Business continuity management systems—Requirements.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch1_v0.9.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('a197a7c7-622c-540e-b4d3-591d2db27aba'::uuid,'2108b48d-63ed-5be3-bdb5-2d1bdabb3811'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# INSTITUTIONAL IMPLEMENTATION PACKAGE\n\nThis package gives operational effect to Chapter 1 through a coordinated set of proposed legislative and executive instruments, standard operating procedures, responsibility matrices and governance templates. It is an integrated annex set and must be read with Chapter 1, particularly Sections 6–8, 11–14.\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL AND APPROVAL\n\n| **Control field**         | **Designation / entry**                                | **Control field**             | **Designation / entry**           |\n|---------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| **Document owner**        | CDRRMO Head                                            | **Document custodian**        | CDRRMO Secretariat                |\n| **Technical review**      | CDRRMO / EOC / concerned offices                       | **Legal review**              | City Legal Office                 |\n| **Administrative review** | HRMO / Budget / Accounting / BAC                       | **External technical review** | OCD Region VIII, as applicable    |\n| **Approval**              | Mayor / CDRRMC / Sanggunian, as legally applicable     | **Effective date**            | Upon valid approval / issuance    |\n| **Review cycle**          | Annual and upon material legal or institutional change | **Supersedes**                | None – initial consolidated draft |\n\n## Revision history\n\n| **Version** | **Date**    | **Description**                                                                                     | **Prepared / reviewed by**            |\n|-------------|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|\n| 0.9         | 31 Oct 2026 | Integrated working draft based on uploaded Chapter 1 and assessment-informed implementation package | Prepared for institutional validation |\n\nApproval status: This document is a controlled working draft. No provision becomes an official City policy, ordinance, executive order or operational directive until approved and issued by the authority legally competent to do so.\n\n# Annex register and cross-reference guide\n\n| **Annex** | **Instrument**                                               | **Approval / control route**           |\n|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| 1-A       | Proposed CDRRMO institutionalization ordinance               | Sangguniang Panlungsod                 |\n| 1-B       | Proposed CDRRMC reconstitution Executive Order               | City Mayor                             |\n| 1-C       | Proposed EOC establishment Executive Order                   | City Mayor                             |\n| 1-D       | SOP GOV-04: State of Calamity declaration and lifting        | Approval under City delegation         |\n| 1-E       | SOP GOV-05: State of Imminent Disaster / anticipatory action | Approval after RA 12287 IRR validation |\n| 1-F       | Master RACI and institutional interface matrix               | CDRRMC / City approval                 |\n| 1-G       | Governance forms GOV-F01 to GOV-F16                          | CDRRMO Secretariat custodian           |\n\nConsistent definitions. CDRRMC means City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council; CDRRMO means permanent City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office; EOC means Emergency Operations Center as an operational coordination mechanism; ICS means Incident Command System; IMT means Incident Management Team; PDRA means Pre-Disaster Risk Assessment; RDANA means Rapid Damage Assessment and Needs Analysis. These definitions are descriptive and do not confer authority beyond applicable law.\n\nDocument hierarchy. Philippine statutes and valid implementing rules prevail over administrative issuances, local executive issuances, approved plans, SOPs and forms. RACI designations allocate work but do not create statutory powers or transfer mandates.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1_00_package_control.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('29770c8d-1104-5794-b2da-cf2a34b631b2'::uuid,'07c8c36f-3092-5f21-8bb0-5df842023f5c'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-A \\| PROPOSED ORDINANCE – CDRRMO INSTITUTIONALIZATION\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## AN ORDINANCE ESTABLISHING AND STRENGTHENING THE CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE OF ORMOC CITY, DEFINING ITS ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURE, FUNCTIONS AND ACCOUNTABILITY, PROVIDING FOR COORDINATION WITH THE CDRRMC AND EOC, AND FOR OTHER PURPOSES\n\nBE IT ORDAINED by the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Ormoc City, in session assembled, that:\n\n## Section 1. Short Title.\n\nThis Ordinance shall be known as the “Ormoc City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office Institutionalization Ordinance.”\n\n## Section 2. Declaration of Policy.\n\nThe City shall maintain a permanent, adequately staffed, technically competent and accountable CDRRMO consistent with RA 10121, its IRR, JMC No. 2014-1 and applicable local government, civil service and budget rules.\n\n## Section 3. Establishment and Status.\n\nThe CDRRMO is hereby established or, if already established by prior valid issuance, strengthened and institutionalized as a regular office of the City Government. The City Legal Office shall determine whether this is a new or amendatory ordinance after reviewing existing issuances.\n\n## Section 4. Organizational Structure.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall be headed by a duly qualified CDRRMO Head and maintain the functional areas required by applicable national guidelines: Administration and Training; Research and Planning; and Operations and Warning. Additional units and positions require approval under DBM, CSC and City processes.\n\n## Section 5. Functions.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall lead and coordinate local DRRM planning and implementation; risk information; contingency planning; early warning; training; preparedness; response coordination; recovery support; CDRRMC Secretariat functions; reporting; and institutional performance improvement, consistent with RA 10121.\n\n## Section 6. Relationship with CDRRMC and EOC.\n\nThe CDRRMC remains the City''s statutory policy-making, coordinating and oversight body. The CDRRMO is its permanent technical and administrative office. The EOC is an operational coordination mechanism established through a separate executive issuance. It does not replace the CDRRMO or field ICS command.\n\n## Section 7. Coordination and non-duplication.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall coordinate with City offices, barangays, national agencies, utilities, CSOs and private-sector partners, but shall not assume technical, regulatory, fiduciary or statutory functions assigned to other offices.\n\n## Section 8. Staffing and qualifications.\n\nHRMO and CDRRMO shall prepare staffing, qualification standards and position descriptions for review under applicable CSC and DBM rules. No position is created beyond an approved plantilla and lawful appropriation.\n\n## Section 9. Funding.\n\nFunding shall be sourced from lawful appropriations and eligible DRRM funds, subject to RA 10121, LDRRMF guidelines, budgeting, accounting, procurement and audit rules. This Ordinance does not independently authorize a particular allocation or expenditure.\n\n## Section 10. Implementing procedures.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain the CDRRMO–EOC Manual of Operations and implementing procedures, subject to legal review and approval by the competent authority.\n\n## Section 11. Separability.\n\nIf any provision is declared invalid, the remaining provisions shall remain effective.\n\n## Section 12. Repealing / amending clause.\n\nInconsistent local issuances are amended or repealed only to the extent of inconsistency, subject to identification by the City Legal Office.\n\n## Section 13. Effectivity.\n\nThis Ordinance shall take effect in accordance with the Local Government Code and applicable approval, posting and publication requirements.\n\nDONE in Ormoc City, Philippines, this \\_\\_\\_\\_ day of \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ 20\\_\\_\\_\\_.  \n  \n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_  \nPRESIDING OFFICER / VICE MAYOR\n\nCERTIFIED CORRECT: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ Secretary to the Sanggunian  \nAPPROVED, if required by law: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ City Mayor\n\nLEGAL REVIEW: Confirm whether an existing ordinance already establishes the CDRRMO; determine new versus amendatory route; align structure and plantilla with JMC 2014-1, DBM/CSC rules; add fiscal note and identify affected prior issuances.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-A.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('532825c5-033c-5bc8-a209-1a4c41bb6e6b'::uuid,'eee45c0c-b84d-5f47-ad35-0d4fab001f7c'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-B \\| PROPOSED EXECUTIVE ORDER – CDRRMC\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## EXECUTIVE ORDER NO. \\_\\_\\_\\_ SERIES OF 20\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n## RECONSTITUTING THE ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT COUNCIL, UPDATING ITS MEMBERSHIP AND ALTERNATES, AND PRESCRIBING ITS GOVERNANCE AND SECRETARIAT ARRANGEMENTS\n\nWHEREAS, RA 10121 and its IRR provide for local DRRM councils and their functions; WHEREAS, the City requires updated representation, regular deliberation and documented follow-through; NOW, THEREFORE, I, \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_, City Mayor of Ormoc City, hereby order:\n\n## Section 1. Reconstitution.\n\nThe CDRRMC is reconstituted in accordance with Section 11 of RA 10121 and its IRR. The final membership shall be listed in Schedule A, completed and legally checked before signature.\n\n## Section 2. Membership and alternates.\n\nEach member institution shall designate a principal and alternate in writing. CDRRMO shall verify all statutory seats, including required accredited CSO representation and private-sector representation, against the Act and IRR.\n\n## Section 3. Functions.\n\nThe Council shall exercise statutory policy, coordination, recommendation, plan review, resource oversight and monitoring functions.\n\n## Section 4. Meetings.\n\nThe Council shall meet at least once every three months and as necessary, consistent with applicable rules.\n\n## Section 5. Secretariat.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall serve as Secretariat and maintain meeting notices, agendas, technical papers, attendance, minutes, resolutions, decision register and action-monitoring reports.\n\n## Section 6. Decision authority.\n\nCouncil recommendations shall be distinguished from decisions reserved by law to the Mayor, Sanggunian or another competent authority.\n\n## Section 7. Working groups and inclusion.\n\nTechnical working groups may be established with terms of reference. CSO, private-sector, barangay and sector participation shall be meaningful and consistent with statutory composition.\n\n## Section 8. Effectivity.\n\nThis Order shall take effect upon signature, subject to applicable law.\n\n| **Schedule A – statutory / institutional seat** | **Principal**                                    | **Alternate** | **Designation / accreditation reference** |\n|-------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|---------------|-------------------------------------------|\n| City Mayor / Chairperson                        | \\[Name\\]                                         | \\[Name\\]      |                                           |\n| Other statutory City offices                    | \\[Complete per RA 10121 / IRR\\]                  |               |                                           |\n| National government agencies                    | \\[Complete per RA 10121 / IRR\\]                  |               |                                           |\n| Accredited CSO representatives                  | \\[At least four, subject to legal verification\\] |               |                                           |\n| Private-sector representative(s)                | \\[Complete\\]                                     |               |                                           |\n| Other prescribed members                        | \\[Complete\\]                                     |               |                                           |\n\nDONE in Ormoc City, Philippines, this \\_\\_\\_\\_ day of \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ 20\\_\\_\\_\\_.  \n  \n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_  \nCITY MAYOR\n\nLEGAL REVIEW: Verify exact statutory seats, CSO accreditation/selection, private-sector membership, alternates, quorum, voting and chair/vice-chair arrangement. Identify prior EO numbers and whether amended or superseded.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-B.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('3252e77f-e003-5ad7-8678-fa69f3aeb2e0'::uuid,'ea2a9893-b34d-56d4-b144-106afe26ed06'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-C \\| PROPOSED EXECUTIVE ORDER – CITY EOC\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## EXECUTIVE ORDER NO. \\_\\_\\_\\_ SERIES OF 20\\_\\_\\_\\_\n\n## ESTABLISHING AND OPERATIONALIZING THE ORMOC CITY EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER, DEFINING ITS FUNCTIONS, ACTIVATION AND DEACTIVATION, AND CLARIFYING ITS INTERFACE WITH THE CDRRMC, CDRRMO AND INCIDENT COMMAND SYSTEM\n\nWHEREAS, RA 10121 requires effective local DRRM coordination; WHEREAS, the City requires a defined operational coordination mechanism; WHEREAS, EOC coordination must not displace CDRRMC/CDRRMO mandates or ICS incident command; NOW, THEREFORE, I, \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_, City Mayor, hereby order:\n\n## Section 1. Establishment.\n\nThe City EOC is established as an operational coordination mechanism administered by the CDRRMO and activated under approved triggers and procedures.\n\n## Section 2. Functions.\n\nThe EOC maintains situational awareness; validates and disseminates incident information; supports executive and CDRRMC decision-making; coordinates resource requests; facilitates inter-office and inter-agency coordination; maintains incident and decision records; and supports transition to recovery.\n\n## Section 3. Non-command status.\n\nThe EOC is not a field command structure. The Incident Commander retains incident-level command under applicable ICS arrangements. EOC coordination does not alter agency mandates or operational control.\n\n## Section 4. Management.\n\nThe CDRRMO Head oversees readiness. A designated EOC Manager manages activated operations under approved job action sheets and reporting lines.\n\n## Section 5. Activation.\n\nActivation may be triggered by official warnings, PDRA findings, actual or imminent impacts, multi-barangay incidents, critical service disruption, resource coordination needs or direction of the Mayor / authorized official. The MOO shall specify levels and authority.\n\n## Section 6. Deactivation.\n\nThe authorized official shall deactivate the EOC when coordination needs have sufficiently reduced and transition arrangements are established. Closure includes logs, resource reconciliation and after-action review.\n\n## Section 7. Readiness and continuity.\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain duty rosters, contact directories, handover procedures, communications redundancy, facility checklists and minimum staffing arrangements, subject to approved resources.\n\n## Section 8. Information and records.\n\nThe EOC shall maintain incident logs, situation reports, decision logs, resource records and dashboards subject to privacy, security, records and disclosure requirements.\n\n## Section 9. Resources and effectivity.\n\nImplementation is subject to approved staffing, appropriations and procurement rules. This Order takes effect upon signature.\n\nDONE in Ormoc City, Philippines, this \\_\\_\\_\\_ day of \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ 20\\_\\_\\_\\_.  \n  \n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_  \nCITY MAYOR\n\nLEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW: Verify NDRRMC MC No. 131, s. 2018 title and current status with OCD Region VIII; identify any existing City EOC issuance; confirm activation/deactivation authority, staffing, facility and ICS/IMT reporting lines.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-C.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('aa041bd5-cd72-5bc2-abe1-d5cccdbc4da7'::uuid,'e715b34c-ec5f-5f4c-b34c-378225a2156c'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-D \\| SOP GOV-04 – STATE OF CALAMITY\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE GOV-04: STATE OF CALAMITY DECLARATION, IMPLEMENTATION AND LIFTING\n\nPurpose. To establish a documented procedure consistent with Section 16 of RA 10121 and its IRR.\n\nCritical authority rule. The local sanggunian declares or lifts a State of Calamity upon recommendation of the LDRRMC, based on damage assessment and needs analysis. A Mayor''s Executive Order shall not substitute for the Sanggunian''s statutory declaration.\n\n| **Control**        | **Requirement**                                                                                                                               |\n|--------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Process owner      | CDRRMO Head                                                                                                                                   |\n| Recommendation     | CDRRMC, based on validated assessment                                                                                                         |\n| Decision authority | Sangguniang Panlungsod, as provided by law                                                                                                    |\n| Core evidence      | RDANA; affected population; casualties; infrastructure / livelihood damage; service interruption; urgent needs; response capacity             |\n| Records            | Assessment, Council minutes / recommendation, Sanggunian resolution, implementation and resource records, lifting recommendation / resolution |\n\n### Procedure\n\nReceive and log reports from BDRRMCs, response agencies, utilities and City offices; record source, time, location and verification status.\n\n1\\. Receive and log reports from BDRRMCs, response agencies, utilities and City offices; record source, time, location and verification status.\n\n2\\. Conduct and consolidate RDANA using approved methodology and competent sector offices; distinguish verified facts, estimates and information gaps.\n\n3\\. Prepare a technical assessment describing scale, severity, affected sectors, urgent needs, available capacity and basis for or against recommending declaration.\n\n4\\. Submit assessment to CDRRMC for deliberation and recommendation where statutory criteria are met.\n\n5\\. Transmit complete recommendation and evidence package to the Sangguniang Panlungsod for action through the legally prescribed process.\n\n6\\. Upon valid Sanggunian resolution, implement authorized remedial measures, assistance and reporting.\n\n7\\. Maintain post-declaration action register and monitor impacts, service restoration, resources and outstanding needs.\n\n8\\. When warranted, recommend lifting; the Sanggunian shall act through the applicable legal process.\n\n9\\. Archive the decision trail and conduct an after-action review.\n\nRequired records: incident report; RDANA; CDRRMC attendance, minutes and recommendation; Sanggunian resolution; action and resource register; public information; lifting recommendation and resolution. CLO shall verify statutory criteria, current IRR, resolution form, publication/posting and lifting process.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-D.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('e0f13a3a-2df6-54bb-a514-03ceebc02c23'::uuid,'f66e432b-487f-5434-a8eb-de42ef848a67'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-E \\| SOP GOV-05 – STATE OF IMMINENT DISASTER\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n## STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE GOV-05: STATE OF IMMINENT DISASTER DECLARATION, ANTICIPATORY ACTION, LIFTING AND POST-LIFTING REPORTING\n\nPurpose. To establish a controlled process under RA 12287 and its certified IRR for PDRA, regional recommendation, local declaration, anticipatory action, lifting and reporting.\n\nCritical authority rule. A forecast alone does not automatically justify declaration. Statutory criteria, PDRA requirements and prescribed recommendation / coordination mechanism must be met. The Mayor''s declaration authority shall be exercised only after all legal prerequisites are confirmed.\n\n| **Control**              | **Requirement**                                                                                            |\n|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Process owner            | CDRRMO Head                                                                                                |\n| Technical lead           | CDRRMO Operations and Warning with sector offices                                                          |\n| Regional interface       | OCD Region VIII / RDRRMC VIII, subject to certified IRR                                                    |\n| Local decision authority | City Mayor, through EO only after prescribed recommendation                                                |\n| Funding                  | Budget / Accounting / Procurement subject to certified IRR and DBM / COA rules                             |\n| Reporting                | Post-lifting deadline as prescribed by certified IRR; 45 days reported in assessment and must be confirmed |\n\n### Procedure\n\n1\\. Monitor official forecasts and advisories from PAGASA, PHIVOLCS and other competent authorities; log source, issue time, validity, geography and lead time.\n\n2\\. Initiate PDRA when hazard-specific triggers warrant; assess exposure, vulnerability, projected severity, cascading impacts, sectoral needs, critical facilities, lead time and response capacity.\n\n3\\. Obtain sector inputs from health, social welfare, agriculture, engineering, utilities, education, transport, environment and barangays as relevant.\n\n4\\. Prepare PDRA summary, proposed anticipatory measures, resource requirements, funding source, implementation owners, safeguards and indicators.\n\n5\\. Convene CDRRMC or its authorized mechanism to review evidence and endorse the City''s request, without substituting for the statutory regional recommendation.\n\n6\\. Coordinate with OCD Region VIII / RDRRMC VIII through the certified IRR route; log request, attachments, receipt, clarifications, recommendation and official response.\n\n7\\. After confirmation of prerequisites, prepare the Mayor''s EO specifying hazard, covered areas, validity, authorized measures, responsible offices, resource controls and reporting.\n\n8\\. Implement only approved and legally authorized actions, which may include prepositioning, early evacuation, critical infrastructure protection, continuity measures and targeted assistance.\n\n9\\. Maintain action logs, beneficiary records, procurement and disbursement records, coverage, outputs, complaints and safeguards. Apply RA 10173 safeguards.\n\n10\\. Monitor hazard and impacts; lift the declaration in the manner prescribed by law and document basis, effective time and transition arrangements.\n\n11\\. Prepare and submit required post-lifting report within deadline in certified IRR; assessment records a 45-day period, which must be verified.\n\n12\\. Conduct after-action review and update triggers, PDRA tools, SOP and corrective action register.\n\nMandatory hold points: CLO verifies certified RA 12287 IRR and recommendation route; OCD VIII confirms regional / provincial coordination applicability to Ormoc as an Independent Component City; Budget, Accounting, CLO and OCD verify reported allocation percentages and tranche rules against certified IRR and DBM/COA guidance; beneficiary data comply with RA 10173.\n\nRequired records: official forecast log; PDRA; sector inputs; CDRRMC records; regional request and acknowledgment; recommendation; Mayor''s EO; anticipatory action / expenditure logs; lifting documentation; post-lifting report; after-action review.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-E.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('37957e8c-b1ea-5311-9e3e-6175b4cce95b'::uuid,'2ad29b21-b115-57cb-8bc8-cc638387a41e'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-F \\| MASTER RACI AND INSTITUTIONAL INTERFACE MATRIX\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n| **Code** | **Meaning** | **Application**                                                                        |\n|----------|-------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| R        | Responsible | Performs work and produces output.                                                     |\n| A        | Accountable | Owns result / decision; one A per activity, subject to collective statutory authority. |\n| C        | Consulted   | Provides required advice before decision.                                              |\n| I        | Informed    | Receives relevant information.                                                         |\n\nRACI does not create statutory authority, transfer legal mandates or override formal delegations. A collective statutory body remains accountable for its decisions.\n\n| **Activity**                         | **Accountable (A)**                         | **Responsible (R)**                   | **Consulted / informed** |\n|--------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| Enact CDRRMO ordinance               | Sangguniang Panlungsod                      | CDRRMO                                | Mayor, CLO, HRMO, Budget |\n| Prepare CDRRMC EO                    | Mayor                                       | CDRRMO / Secretariat                  | CLO, CDRRMC              |\n| Maintain CDRRMC register             | CDRRMO Head                                 | Secretariat                           | CLO, Council             |\n| Prepare CDRRMC meetings              | CDRRMC                                      | Secretariat                           | CDRRMO, member offices   |\n| Monitor Council actions              | CDRRMC                                      | CDRRMO / Secretariat                  | Concerned offices        |\n| Prepare State of Calamity assessment | CDRRMO Head                                 | RDANA / sector teams                  | CDRRMC, CLO, Finance     |\n| Recommend State of Calamity          | CDRRMC                                      | CDRRMO supplies evidence              | Sanggunian, CLO          |\n| Declare / lift State of Calamity     | Sangguniang Panlungsod                      | Sanggunian Secretariat                | CDRRMC, Mayor, CLO       |\n| Prepare SID PDRA                     | CDRRMO Head                                 | Operations & Warning / sector offices | CDRRMC, CLO, Budget, OCD |\n| Submit SID request                   | Authority prescribed by certified IRR       | CDRRMO                                | OCD VIII, CLO            |\n| Regional recommendation              | Competent regional body under certified IRR | OCD / RDRRMC VIII secretariat         | City                     |\n| Issue local SID EO                   | Mayor                                       | CDRRMO prepares package               | CLO, Budget, CDRRMC      |\n| Implement anticipatory action        | Mayor / designated office as authorized     | Sector offices / CDRRMO coordination  | Budget, Accounting, OCD  |\n| Prepare post-lifting report          | CDRRMO Head                                 | CDRRMO / sector offices               | CLO, Budget, OCD         |\n| Maintain legal register              | CDRRMO Head                                 | Secretariat / records custodian       | CLO                      |\n| Annual governance review             | CDRRMC                                      | CDRRMO / Secretariat                  | CLO, HRMO, Budget, OCD   |\n\n| **Interface**                           | **Accountable owner**                        | **Responsible actors**         | **CDRRMO role**                                  |\n|-----------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRA / CLUP integration                 | CPDO                                         | CPDO technical team            | CDRRMO provides risk evidence and DRR/CCA advice |\n| DRR/CCA project screening               | CPDO / Budget by function                    | CPDO and Budget                | CDRRMO provides risk-screening inputs            |\n| Critical facility structural assessment | City Engineer / Building Official            | Engineering / inspection teams | CDRRMO supports risk prioritization              |\n| Power, water, telecom continuity        | Utility provider / designated City authority | Utilities and relevant offices | CDRRMO coordinates information and priorities    |\n| Watershed / ecosystem monitoring        | CENRO / competent environment authority      | CENRO, DENR and partners       | CDRRMO integrates findings                       |\n| Health anticipatory action              | City Health Officer                          | CHO, hospitals and partners    | CDRRMO coordinates through EOC / cluster         |\n| Education continuity                    | DepEd Schools Division                       | School DRRM coordinators       | CDRRMO coordinates preparedness                  |\n| Agriculture / fisheries early action    | City Agriculture / Veterinary Office         | Sector offices and agencies    | CDRRMO coordinates hazard information            |\n| Price monitoring                        | DTI / City Market Office                     | Market monitoring personnel    | EOC information integration                      |\n| Business continuity outreach            | CDRRMO Head                                  | CDRRMO and Chamber of Commerce | Joint outreach; each retains own mandate         |\n\nAll office heads shall validate assignments. CDRRMO coordinates system-wide resilience but does not appropriate statutory or technical mandates belonging to other institutions.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-F.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft'),
('df391a4d-2377-55d2-a59f-1c37ae577ad2'::uuid,'1a2135aa-84f2-5ac2-97ff-f5ba295fcf32'::uuid,'v0.9 consolidated draft',E'# ANNEX 1-G \\| GOVERNANCE FORMS, TEMPLATES AND REGISTERS\n\n**PROPOSED – FOR LEGAL / TECHNICAL REVIEW**\n\nDocument status: Draft instrument. This text is a drafting aid and must be checked against the latest certified statutes, implementing rules, local organizational structure, approved plantilla, budget, and applicable City procedures before signature or enactment.\n\n| **Form code** | **Template / register**                          | **Minimum fields**                                                                                                                   |\n|---------------|--------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| GOV-F01       | CDRRMC Membership and Designation Register       | Statutory seat; institution; principal; alternate; designation date; CSO accreditation; contact; validity; verification.             |\n| GOV-F02       | CDRRMC Meeting Notice and Agenda                 | Meeting number; date/time/venue; convening authority; agenda; lead office; papers; distribution.                                     |\n| GOV-F03       | CDRRMC Attendance and Minutes                    | Attendance; quorum; agenda; discussion; decisions; dissent; action owner; deadline; approval.                                        |\n| GOV-F04       | CDRRMC Resolution Template                       | Resolution number/title; legal basis; findings; operative clauses; voting; certification.                                            |\n| GOV-F05       | Resolution and Action Monitoring Register        | Decision; action owner; deliverable; due date; status; evidence; escalation; closure.                                                |\n| GOV-F06       | Institutional Policy Review Checklist            | Legal authority; consistency; mandate; consultation; inclusion; privacy; staffing; budget; monitoring; legal clearance.              |\n| GOV-F07       | State of Calamity Technical Assessment           | Event; affected barangays; casualties; population; damage; livelihood; services; needs; capacity; evidence.                          |\n| GOV-F08       | State of Calamity Recommendation and Resolution  | RDANA; CDRRMC deliberation; recommendation; Sanggunian action; legal review; implementation / lifting.                               |\n| GOV-F09       | State of Imminent Disaster PDRA Summary          | Hazard; official source; forecast; lead time; geography; exposure; vulnerability; impacts; sector needs; actions; costs; safeguards. |\n| GOV-F10       | Request to RDRRMC VIII / OCD VIII                | Legal route; requesting authority; PDRA; action proposal; attachments; submission; acknowledgment; response.                         |\n| GOV-F11       | Mayor''s State of Imminent Disaster EO Template   | Recitals; legal basis; prerequisite recommendation; hazard / areas; actions; owners; resources; duration; reporting; lifting.        |\n| GOV-F12       | State of Imminent Disaster Lifting Documentation | Updated risk; basis; regional coordination; authority; lifting instrument; effective time; transition.                               |\n| GOV-F13       | Post-Lifting Report                              | Timeline; PDRA; actions; coverage; expenditure; beneficiaries; outputs; lessons; attachments; proof of submission.                   |\n| GOV-F14       | Institutional RACI Register                      | Process; activity; A; R; C; I; legal authority; approving authority; review date.                                                    |\n| GOV-F15       | Corrective and Preventive Action Register        | Finding; root cause; risk; action; owner; actors; resources; due date; evidence; effectiveness; closure.                             |\n| GOV-F16       | Legal and Policy Register                        | Instrument; authority; number/date; subject; status; official source; owner; implications; review date.                              |\n\nEvery controlled form shall display City Government of Ormoc, document code, version, date, page number, preparer, reviewer, approving authority where applicable, classification, records custodian and related SOP / Chapter reference. Personal information shall be collected only when necessary and processed under RA 10173.\n\n# FINAL INTEGRATION AND RELEASE CHECKLIST\n\n| **Validation subject**     | **Required action**                                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMO legal basis         | CLO to identify existing ordinances / EOs and determine new or amendatory legislative route.                                                          |\n| Structure and staffing     | HRMO / CDRRMO to align functions, plantilla and qualifications with JMC 2014-1 and current DBM / CSC rules.                                           |\n| CDRRMC membership          | Verify statutory seats, CSO accreditation / selection, private-sector representation, alternates, quorum and voting.                                  |\n| EOC basis                  | OCD VIII / CLO to verify national EOC guidance, existing City orders, activation authority and ICS interface.                                         |\n| State of Calamity          | CLO to validate RA 10121 Section 16, evidence criteria, Sanggunian action and lifting process.                                                        |\n| State of Imminent Disaster | CLO / OCD VIII to verify certified RA 12287 IRR, regional route, Ormoc ICC coordination, timelines and local EO prerequisites.                        |\n| Funding                    | Budget / Accounting / BAC / CLO to verify percentages, eligibility, tranche rules and procurement / disbursement against certified IRR and DBM / COA. |\n| Privacy and records        | DPO / ICT / Records Officer to validate beneficiary data, data sharing, retention, access and security.                                               |\n| Document consistency       | Secretariat to check annex codes, form codes, cross-references, register and revision history.                                                        |\n\nRelease condition: No draft instrument shall be signed, enacted, circulated as effective policy or used as the sole basis for expenditure until its legal route, factual schedules, delegated authority, budget implications and approval requirements have been confirmed.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/Annex_1-G.md (Chapter 1 consolidated draft v0.9, 31 Oct 2026)','draft')
